INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Setting VALUES(1,'WiseCompare','Independent comparisons for smarter decisions','','WiseCompare is an independent publication that compares software, financial tools and home insurance options. We may earn a commission from partner links; this never affects our editorial ratings. Content is for general information only and is not financial, legal or insurance advice.','hello@wisecompareguide.com','',replace('Ranganath Mutyala\nHyderabad, Telangana, India','\n',char(10)),'','','2026-11-24T05:59:32.357+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Page VALUES('cmwuabpcb00007ds0969cz68w','about','About WiseCompare','WiseCompare is an independent comparison site run by Ranganath Mutyala. Learn who we are, how we research and rate products, and how we stay independent.',replace('WiseCompare (wisecompareguide.com) is an independent comparison and review website. It helps everyday consumers and small businesses make better decisions about software, money tools and their homes. We publish side-by-side comparisons, in-depth reviews and plain-English guides in three areas:\n\n- **AI & productivity software:** AI assistants, meeting tools, automation and collaboration apps.\n- **Personal finance & credit tools:** credit monitoring, budgeting apps and other money-management services.\n- **Home services & insurance:** homeowners insurance, home warranties and related services.\n\n## Meet the team\n\nWiseCompare is run by a small team based in Hyderabad, Telangana, India:\n\n- **Ranganath Mutyala**, Founder & Editor\n- **Suresh Gottapu**, Co-Founder\n- **Niteesh Manne**, CEO\n\n### Why we started WiseCompare\n\nFounder Ranganath Mutyala has a professional background in data and cloud technology. His day-to-day work involves keeping business systems reliable, secure and well-documented. He started WiseCompare after seeing how hard it is for ordinary buyers to compare products honestly: pricing pages hide the real cost, free trials quietly turn into subscriptions, and many "reviews" are really advertisements.\n\nHis approach is simple: read the fine print, compare products on the things that actually matter to the buyer, and explain the trade-offs clearly, including the downsides.\n\n## How we research and rate products\n\nEvery comparison and review follows the same process:\n\n1. **Start with the reader''s problem.** We begin with what the reader is trying to achieve (for example, "summarise client calls automatically" or "get a fair home insurance quote") and decide which criteria matter for that job.\n2. **Go to primary sources.** We review each provider''s own pricing pages, terms of service, privacy policy and help documentation. Where practical, we sign up for free plans or trials.\n3. **Score against clear criteria.** Ratings on a 0–5 scale reflect value for money, features, ease of use, transparency of pricing and terms, privacy and security, and customer support.\n4. **Always show the downsides.** Every review includes limitations and a "watch out for" list. If a product isn''t right for a certain type of user, we say so.\n5. **Keep it current.** We re-check prices and key terms regularly and show a "last updated" date on each page. Prices and promotions change often, so always confirm current terms on the provider''s website.\n\n## Editorial independence\n\nWiseCompare is free to read. We earn money through affiliate partnerships: if you click certain links and then sign up or buy, we may receive a commission, at no extra cost to you. Partners cannot pay for a better rating, a more favourable review or the removal of negative information. You can read exactly how this works in our [Affiliate Disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure).\n\n## What we don''t do\n\n- We don''t give personalised financial, legal, tax or insurance advice. Our content is general information to help you compare options. For decisions about your own situation, speak to a licensed professional.\n- We are not a lender, insurer, broker or software vendor, and we don''t make credit or underwriting decisions.\n- We don''t sell your personal information.\n\n## Corrections and feedback\n\nAccuracy matters to us. If you spot an error or something out of date, please [contact us](/contact) and we will review and correct it promptly.\n\n## Contact\n\n- **Ranganath Mutyala**, Founder & Editor\n- **Suresh Gottapu**, Co-Founder\n- **Niteesh Manne**, CEO\n- Hyderabad, Telangana, India\n- Email: [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com)','\n',char(10)),'2026-11-24T05:59:32.363+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Page VALUES('cmwuabpce00017ds0kjz3p830','contact','Contact Us','Contact WiseCompare for corrections, reader questions, partnerships or privacy requests. We reply within two business days.',replace('We''d love to hear from you. WiseCompare is run by a small team in Hyderabad, Telangana, India, and every message is read personally.\n\n**Our team**\n\n- **Ranganath Mutyala**, Founder & Editor\n- **Suresh Gottapu**, Co-Founder\n- **Niteesh Manne**, CEO\n\n**How to reach us**\n\n- **Email:** [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com)\n- **Location:** Hyderabad, Telangana, India\n- **Response time:** within 2 business days\n\n## What you can contact us about\n\n- **Corrections:** if something in one of our articles looks wrong or out of date.\n- **Reader questions:** we can''t give personal financial or insurance advice, but your questions help us decide which guides to write next.\n- **Partnerships & advertising:** brands and affiliate networks are welcome to get in touch. Please note that commercial partners cannot influence our ratings or editorial content.\n- **Privacy requests:** to access, correct or delete personal data we hold about you (see our [Privacy Policy](/privacy-policy)).\n\nYou can also use the form below.','\n',char(10)),'2026-11-24T05:59:32.366+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Page VALUES('cmwuabpcg00027ds0dibvobfy','privacy-policy','Privacy & Cookie Policy','How WiseCompare collects, uses and protects personal information, how we use cookies, and your rights under GDPR, CCPA/CPRA and India''s DPDP Act.',replace('This Privacy & Cookie Policy explains how WiseCompare (wisecompareguide.com) collects, uses and protects personal information. WiseCompare is operated by **Ranganath Mutyala**, Hyderabad, Telangana, India ("we", "us", "our"). We are responsible for (the "data controller" or "data fiduciary" of) personal information collected through this website.\n\nThis policy applies to all visitors, and includes information for residents of the European Economic Area and UK (GDPR / UK GDPR), California (CCPA as amended by the CPRA) and India (Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023).\n\n**Contact for privacy questions:** [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com)\n\n## Information we collect\n\n**Information you give us**\n\n- **Email address:** if you subscribe through an email signup box, together with the page you signed up on, the time of signup and your consent.\n- **Contact form details:** your name, email address and message when you contact us.\n\n**Information collected automatically**\n\n- **Technical and usage data:** such as pages visited, referring website, approximate location derived from your IP address, browser type and device type. Our hosting provider processes this to deliver and secure the site.\n- **Advertising and campaign parameters:** when you arrive from an online advertisement, the web address may contain identifiers such as a Google click ID (gclid) or UTM campaign tags. When you click a link to a partner, these may be passed to our click-tracking provider and the partner so that visits and sign-ups can be attributed correctly.\n- **Cookies and similar technologies:** see "Cookies" below.\n\n## How we use your information\n\n- To operate, maintain, secure and improve the website.\n- To send you the content you requested and, if you opted in, occasional emails. You can unsubscribe at any time.\n- To reply to your messages.\n- To measure how our content and advertising perform, including which partner links are clicked and whether a referral led to a sign-up or purchase.\n- To prevent spam, fraud and abuse, and to meet legal obligations.\n\n**Legal bases (GDPR):** your consent (email subscriptions, non-essential cookies), our legitimate interests (site security, basic performance measurement, replying to enquiries) and legal obligations.\n\n## Affiliate links and tracking\n\nSome links on this site are affiliate links. When you click one, our click-tracking platform and the partner''s website (or the affiliate network that manages the partnership) may set cookies or receive referral data, such as a click identifier, so that the partner can credit us for the referral. These companies process data under their own privacy policies. We never receive your payment details or application data from partners.\n\n## Who we share information with\n\nWe do not sell your personal information. We share it only with:\n\n- **Service providers** that help us run the site: website hosting, security and email forwarding (Cloudflare), and click tracking for affiliate links. They may only use the data to provide their services to us.\n- **Affiliate partners and networks**, limited to the referral and attribution data described above.\n- **Authorities**, where required by law.\n\n## Cookies\n\n| Type | Purpose | Your choice |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Essential | Remember your cookie choice and keep the site secure | Always on; required for the site to work |\n| Performance | Understand which pages are useful | Optional |\n| Advertising / affiliate | Attribute ad clicks and referrals to partners | Optional |\n\nYou can choose **"Essential only"** in our cookie banner, block cookies in your browser settings, or delete cookies at any time. Blocking some cookies may affect how parts of the site work.\n\n## How long we keep information\n\n- Email subscriber data: until you unsubscribe or ask us to delete it.\n- Contact messages: up to 24 months.\n- Technical logs: kept only for a limited period by our hosting provider for security and troubleshooting.\n\n## Your rights\n\nDepending on where you live, you may have the right to:\n\n- access the personal data we hold about you and receive a copy;\n- correct inaccurate data, or have your data deleted;\n- object to or restrict certain processing;\n- withdraw your consent at any time (this doesn''t affect processing done before you withdrew);\n- complain to your local data protection authority.\n\n**California residents** have the right to know what personal information we collect, to request deletion and correction, to opt out of the "sale" or "sharing" of personal information, and not to be discriminated against for exercising these rights. We do not sell personal information, and we honour Global Privacy Control (GPC) signals where technically feasible.\n\n**Residents of India** may exercise their rights under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, including access, correction, erasure and grievance redressal. Our grievance contact is Ranganath Mutyala, at [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com).\n\nTo make any request, email [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com). We may need to verify your identity, and we will respond within the time required by applicable law.\n\n## International transfers\n\nWe are based in India, and our service providers may process data in other countries, including the United States. Where required, transfers are protected by appropriate safeguards such as Standard Contractual Clauses.\n\n## Children\n\nThis website is not directed to children under 16 (or under 18 in India), and we do not knowingly collect their personal information. If you believe a child has given us personal data, please contact us and we will delete it.\n\n## Security\n\nWe use reasonable technical and organisational measures to protect personal information, including encrypted connections (HTTPS) and restricted, password-protected administrative access. No method of transmission over the internet is completely secure.\n\n## Changes to this policy\n\nWe may update this policy from time to time. The "last updated" date at the top of this page shows when it was last revised.\n\n## Contact us\n\n- **Ranganath Mutyala**, WiseCompare\n- Hyderabad, Telangana, India\n- Email: [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com)','\n',char(10)),'2026-11-24T05:59:32.369+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Page VALUES('cmwuabpci00037ds0b48irtrh','affiliate-disclosure','Affiliate & Advertiser Disclosure','How WiseCompare makes money: our affiliate relationships, how they may affect where products appear, and how we keep our ratings independent.',replace('WiseCompare is a free, reader-supported publication. To keep it free, we participate in affiliate and advertising programs. This page explains what that means, in line with the U.S. Federal Trade Commission''s Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements and Testimonials in Advertising and similar rules in other countries.\n\n## The short version\n\n- **Some links on this site are affiliate links.** If you click one and then sign up, request a quote or make a purchase, we may earn a commission.\n- **It costs you nothing extra.** The price you pay is the same whether or not you use our link.\n- **Partners can''t buy our opinion.** Ratings, pros and cons and editorial verdicts are written by our editorial team using published criteria.\n\n## Which pages contain affiliate links?\n\nAny page with outbound "Check availability", "View offer", "Get quotes" or similar buttons may contain affiliate links. Pages with affiliate links show a short disclosure banner at the top. Buttons and links that lead to partners are marked as sponsored links in the page code.\n\n## How compensation may affect what you see\n\nCompensation may influence **which** products we feature and **where and how** they appear on a page, including the order in comparison tables. It does **not** influence our ratings or the content of our reviews. We do not cover every product or company available in the market.\n\n## How we keep ratings independent\n\n- Ratings are based on weighted editorial criteria described on our [About](/about) page.\n- Commercial partners do not review or approve editorial content before publication.\n- We include limitations and downsides for every product we feature, including those we earn commission from.\n\n## Tracking\n\nWhen you click an affiliate link, a tracking platform records the click so the partner can credit the referral. This may involve cookies and campaign parameters such as click identifiers. See our [Privacy & Cookie Policy](/privacy-policy) for details and choices.\n\n## Not professional advice\n\nOur content is for general information only and is not financial, legal, tax or insurance advice. Insurance quotes, credit products and software pricing depend on your circumstances and may change. Always read the provider''s terms before you sign up.\n\n## Questions\n\nWiseCompare is operated by Ranganath Mutyala, Hyderabad, Telangana, India. If you have questions about this disclosure, please [contact us](/contact).','\n',char(10)),'2026-11-24T05:59:32.370+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Page VALUES('cmwuabpck00047ds0mv4r5hfw','terms','Terms of Use','The terms that apply when you use WiseCompare (wisecompareguide.com): information only, affiliate links, third-party sites, intellectual property and liability.',replace('These Terms of Use apply to your use of WiseCompare (wisecompareguide.com), operated by **Ranganath Mutyala**, Hyderabad, Telangana, India ("we", "us", "our"). By using this website, you agree to these terms. If you don''t agree, please don''t use the site.\n\n## 1. Information only, not professional advice\n\nThe content on WiseCompare (comparisons, reviews, ratings and guides) is general information to help you compare products and services. It is **not** financial, investment, legal, tax, insurance or other professional advice, and it doesn''t take your personal circumstances into account. Before making a decision, read the provider''s own terms and, where appropriate, speak to a qualified professional.\n\n## 2. Accuracy and availability\n\nWe work hard to keep information accurate and up to date, but prices, features, promotions and terms change frequently and may differ from what we describe. We don''t guarantee that the content is complete, current or error-free, and we may change or remove content at any time. Always confirm details on the provider''s website before you buy or sign up. If you spot an error, please [contact us](/contact).\n\n## 3. Affiliate links and third-party websites\n\nSome links on this site are affiliate links, which means we may earn a commission if you click them and make a purchase or sign up, at no extra cost to you. See our [Affiliate Disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure) for details.\n\nLinks to third-party websites are provided for convenience. We don''t control those websites and aren''t responsible for their content, products, services, privacy practices or availability. Any purchase or agreement you make with a third party is solely between you and that third party.\n\n## 4. Ratings and opinions\n\nRatings and verdicts reflect our editorial opinion based on the criteria described on our [About](/about) page at the time of review. Your experience may differ.\n\n## 5. Acceptable use\n\nYou agree not to:\n\n- use the site for any unlawful purpose or in breach of these terms;\n- attempt to gain unauthorised access to the site, its servers or administrative areas;\n- interfere with the site''s operation, including through malware, scraping that overloads the site, or automated spam submissions;\n- copy or republish substantial parts of our content without permission.\n\n## 6. Intellectual property\n\nThe WiseCompare name, logo, website design and original content are owned by us and protected by intellectual property laws. You may view and share links to our pages, and quote short extracts with a link back to the source. Third-party trademarks and product names belong to their respective owners and are used for identification only.\n\n## 7. Your submissions\n\nIf you send us a message or sign up for emails, you confirm that the information you provide is accurate and that you''re entitled to provide it. We handle personal information as described in our [Privacy & Cookie Policy](/privacy-policy).\n\n## 8. Limitation of liability\n\nTo the fullest extent permitted by law, WiseCompare and its team are not liable for any loss or damage arising from your use of, or reliance on, this website or any third-party website, product or service linked from it. Nothing in these terms excludes liability that cannot be excluded under applicable law.\n\n## 9. Changes to these terms\n\nWe may update these terms from time to time. The "last updated" date at the top of this page shows when they were last revised. Continuing to use the site means you accept the updated terms.\n\n## 10. Governing law\n\nThese terms are governed by the laws of India. Subject to any mandatory consumer protection rights you have in your country of residence, disputes are subject to the jurisdiction of the courts of Hyderabad, Telangana.\n\n## 11. Contact\n\n- **Ranganath Mutyala**, WiseCompare\n- Hyderabad, Telangana, India\n- Email: [hello@wisecompareguide.com](mailto:hello@wisecompareguide.com)','\n',char(10)),'2026-11-24T05:59:32.372+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO LandingPage VALUES('cmwuabpcp00057ds03ar28i4b','best-ai-meeting-assistant','AI Meeting Assistant — NoteLoop (sample)','ai-saas','published','Best AI Meeting Note-Taker for Small Teams (2026 Comparison)','Compare AI meeting assistants side by side: transcription accuracy, CRM sync, privacy controls and pricing. See which tool fits a small team.','NoteLoop AI — Free Trial','https://example.bemobtrk.com/click?sample=noteloop','','head',1,45.0,'["US","UK","CA","AU"]','SAMPLE PAGE — replace CTA URL with your BeMob campaign click URL and verify every claim with the advertiser.','Updated for 2026','The AI Meeting Assistant That Turns Calls Into Action Items — Automatically','We compared popular AI note-takers on accuracy, privacy controls and integrations. Here''s the one we''d pick for a 5–50 person team, and when an alternative makes more sense.','["Joins Zoom, Google Meet and Microsoft Teams calls and writes the summary for you","Pushes action items and notes to your CRM or project tool","Admin controls for recording consent and data retention"]','Start the Free Trial','Free plan available · Cancel anytime on paid plans','AI meeting assistants compared','["Best For","Pricing / Free Trial","Key Advantage"]','[{"name":"NoteLoop AI","tagline":"Sample offer","highlight":true,"rating":4.7,"cells":["Client-facing teams of 5–50","Free plan; paid from ~$15/user/mo","CRM sync + consent controls"],"ctaLabel":"Try free"},{"name":"MinuteMate","tagline":"Sample alternative","highlight":false,"rating":4.3,"cells":["Solo consultants","Free tier; paid from ~$10/mo","Simple, fast summaries"],"ctaLabel":"View offer"},{"name":"Built-in video platform recaps","tagline":"Zoom / Meet / Teams add-ons","highlight":false,"rating":3.9,"cells":["Teams already on one platform","Often bundled with business plans","No extra vendor to approve"],"ctaLabel":"Compare"}]',replace('## How an AI meeting assistant works\n\nAn AI meeting assistant connects to your calendar and joins scheduled video calls as a participant (or records locally on your device). During the call it captures audio and produces a transcript. After the call it uses a language model to turn that transcript into a short summary, a list of decisions, and action items with owners. The better tools then push that output to where your team already works — a CRM record, a project board, a shared doc or a Slack channel — so nobody has to copy and paste notes.\n\nThe practical result: instead of one person taking notes while half-listening, everyone can participate, and the follow-up email writes itself.\n\n## Who NoteLoop AI is for\n\nNoteLoop is built for **small client-facing teams** — agencies, sales teams, recruiters, customer success and consultants — who run several external calls a day and need clean records afterwards. It''s a strong fit if:\n\n- You use a CRM and want call notes attached to the right contact or deal automatically.\n- Several people need to see call notes without attending every call.\n- You need admin control over who can record, how participants are notified, and how long recordings are kept.\n\nIt''s **less suited** to teams that mainly hold short internal stand-ups (a built-in recap from your video platform may be enough) or to organisations with strict on-premises requirements.\n\n## What stood out in our evaluation\n\n**Summary quality.** Summaries were structured into topics, decisions and next steps rather than a wall of text, which made them genuinely usable as follow-up notes. As with every AI tool, summaries occasionally missed nuance or attributed a comment to the wrong speaker, so we recommend a quick skim before you share notes with a client.\n\n**Integrations.** CRM and project-tool sync is the main reason to choose a dedicated assistant over a built-in recap. Notes land on the right record without manual work, which is where most of the time saving comes from.\n\n**Consent and privacy controls.** Recording laws vary: some jurisdictions require every participant''s consent. NoteLoop lets admins enforce an automatic recording notice, restrict which meetings can be recorded, and set retention periods. Before you buy any AI note-taker, check where data is stored, whether your recordings are used to train models, and whether the vendor offers a data processing agreement.\n\n**Pricing.** There''s a free plan for trying it out, with paid per-user plans unlocking integrations and admin controls. Always confirm current pricing on the vendor''s site — AI tool pricing changes frequently.\n\n## How it compares with the alternatives\n\n**MinuteMate** is simpler and cheaper, and a good choice for a solo consultant who just wants a quick summary emailed after each call. It has fewer admin and integration options, so it becomes limiting as a team grows.\n\n**Built-in recaps** from Zoom, Google Meet or Microsoft Teams avoid adding another vendor and may already be included in your business plan. The trade-off is that they generally work only inside that one platform and offer more limited CRM integration. If your team uses multiple meeting platforms or needs notes in a CRM, a dedicated tool is usually worth it.\n\n## How to get the most from a free trial\n\n1. Connect your calendar and run the assistant on five to ten real calls, not test calls.\n2. Turn on the CRM or project-tool integration on day one — that''s where you''ll feel the difference.\n3. Configure the recording notice before you use it with clients.\n4. At the end of the trial, compare the time your team spent on notes before and after.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nIf your team spends hours each week writing up calls and updating a CRM, a dedicated AI meeting assistant pays for itself quickly. NoteLoop combines solid summaries with the integrations and consent controls that matter for client-facing work. If you only need occasional internal recaps, start with what your video platform already offers.','\n',char(10)),'["Structured summaries with decisions and action items, not just transcripts","Automatic CRM and project-tool sync saves the most time","Admin controls for recording notices and retention","Free plan to test on real calls before paying"]','["AI summaries still need a quick human check before sharing externally","Integrations and admin features require a paid plan","Overkill for teams that mostly hold short internal meetings"]','For small client-facing teams that live in their CRM, NoteLoop AI is our top pick: reliable summaries, the integrations that actually save time, and sensible privacy controls. Try it on a week of real calls before committing.',1,1,1,'Free: AI Meeting Assistant Security Checklist','12 questions to ask any vendor before you let a bot record client calls. One email, no spam.','2026-11-24T05:59:32.377+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.377+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO LandingPage VALUES('cmwuabpct00067ds0jv8qkdll','credit-monitoring-service','Credit Monitoring — ScoreShield (sample)','finance','published','Best Credit Monitoring Service: 3-Bureau Alerts Compared (2026)','Is paid credit monitoring worth it? Compare 3-bureau monitoring, identity theft insurance and free alternatives like credit freezes and free weekly reports.','ScoreShield — 3-Bureau Monitoring','https://example.bemobtrk.com/click?sample=scoreshield','','head',1,30.0,'["US"]','SAMPLE PAGE — US-only. Confirm the advertiser''s trial terms and identity-insurance amounts before going live; Google Ads financial services policies may require advertiser verification.','Updated for 2026','Get Alerted the Moment Something Changes on Your Credit Report','Paid credit monitoring isn''t for everyone. We compare 3-bureau monitoring with the free tools every US consumer can use — and explain when paying makes sense.','["Alerts for new accounts, hard inquiries and address changes at all three bureaus","Identity restoration support if something goes wrong","Clear monthly pricing — know exactly what you''ll pay before you start"]','See Plans & Pricing','Checking your own credit does not affect your score','Credit monitoring options compared','["Best For","Pricing / Free Trial","Key Advantage"]','[{"name":"ScoreShield","tagline":"Sample offer","highlight":true,"rating":4.6,"cells":["Hands-off 3-bureau protection","Monthly plan; check site for trial terms","Alerts + identity restoration"],"ctaLabel":"See plans"},{"name":"Free bank / card score tools","tagline":"Included with many accounts","highlight":false,"rating":4,"cells":["Tracking your score trend","Free","Already in your banking app"],"ctaLabel":"Learn more"},{"name":"Credit freeze + free weekly reports","tagline":"DIY at each bureau","highlight":false,"rating":4.2,"cells":["Blocking new-account fraud","Free by law","Strongest prevention, no cost"],"ctaLabel":"Compare"}]',replace('## What credit monitoring actually does\n\nA credit monitoring service watches your credit reports at Equifax, Experian and TransUnion and alerts you when something changes — a new account is opened, a lender makes a hard inquiry, your address changes, or a collection account appears. The value is **speed**: the sooner you know about an account you didn''t open, the faster you can dispute it and limit the damage.\n\nMonitoring is a **detection** tool. It doesn''t stop someone from applying for credit in your name; it tells you after it happens. That distinction matters when you compare it with free alternatives.\n\n## Who a paid service like ScoreShield is for\n\nPaid 3-bureau monitoring is worth considering if:\n\n- Your personal data has been exposed in a data breach and you want ongoing alerts across all three bureaus.\n- You''re about to apply for a mortgage or car loan and want to spot errors early.\n- You want someone else to handle the paperwork if identity theft does happen — many paid plans include identity restoration specialists and an identity theft insurance policy that covers certain expenses.\n- You prefer a single dashboard to managing three bureau accounts yourself.\n\nIt''s **probably not necessary** if you already have a credit freeze in place, check your free reports regularly, and are comfortable handling disputes yourself.\n\n## The free alternatives you should know about\n\n**Credit freezes are free.** Since 2018, US federal law requires all three bureaus to let you freeze and unfreeze your credit at no charge. A freeze stops most lenders from accessing your report, which blocks the majority of new-account fraud. You temporarily lift it when you want to apply for credit.\n\n**Free credit reports.** You can get free credit reports from each of the three bureaus through AnnualCreditReport.com, the official site authorised by federal law. The bureaus now offer these free reports weekly.\n\n**Bank and card tools.** Many banks and card issuers show a free credit score and basic change alerts, usually from one bureau.\n\nFor many people, a freeze plus regular report checks provides strong protection at zero cost. Paid monitoring adds convenience, three-bureau coverage in one place, and restoration support.\n\n## What we looked for\n\n- **Three-bureau coverage**, not just one.\n- **Alert speed and clarity** — alerts you can understand and act on.\n- **Identity restoration** — access to specialists who help you file disputes and police reports.\n- **Transparent pricing** — clear monthly cost, clear trial terms, easy cancellation.\n- **Privacy** — what the service does with your data, and whether it''s shared for marketing.\n\n## Our take on ScoreShield\n\nScoreShield covers all three bureaus, sends clear alerts for the changes that matter most, and includes identity restoration support. Its main appeal is convenience: everything in one dashboard and help on hand if something goes wrong.\n\nBefore you sign up, read the trial terms carefully. Some monitoring services start with a low-cost or free trial that converts to a monthly subscription; set a reminder if you only want to try it. Also check the exact identity theft insurance limits and exclusions in the policy documents rather than relying on headline figures.\n\n## A sensible setup\n\n1. Freeze your credit at all three bureaus (free).\n2. Pull your free reports and check them for errors.\n3. If you want three-bureau alerts and restoration help without the DIY work, add a paid monitoring service like ScoreShield.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nCredit monitoring is a convenience and peace-of-mind product, not a magic shield. If you value hands-off alerts across all three bureaus and expert help if your identity is stolen, ScoreShield is a solid option. If you''re comfortable with DIY, a free credit freeze and regular free reports go a long way.','\n',char(10)),'["Monitors all three bureaus in a single dashboard","Identity restoration specialists included","Checking your own credit doesn''t affect your score","Useful before major applications like a mortgage"]','["Detects fraud after the fact — a free credit freeze prevents more of it","Monthly cost adds up compared with free tools","Trial terms can auto-renew — read them before you start"]','If you want hands-off, three-bureau alerts and someone to call if your identity is stolen, ScoreShield is worth a look. Pair it with a free credit freeze for the strongest protection.',1,0,1,'Free: Identity Theft Recovery Checklist','Step-by-step actions to take in the first 24 hours.','2026-11-24T05:59:32.381+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.381+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO LandingPage VALUES('cmwuabpcw00077ds0wy51rhmd','compare-home-insurance-quotes','Home Insurance Quotes — QuoteNest (sample)','home-insurance','published','Compare Home Insurance Quotes in Minutes (2026 Guide)','Compare homeowners insurance quotes from multiple insurers in one place. Learn what affects your premium and how to compare coverage, not just price.','QuoteNest — Home Insurance Quotes','https://example.bemobtrk.com/click?sample=quotenest','','head',1,18.0,'["US"]','SAMPLE PAGE — insurance lead-gen; confirm the advertiser is licensed in target states and that their consent language complies with TCPA rules.','Updated for 2026','Compare Home Insurance Quotes From Multiple Insurers — Without the Sales Calls Marathon','Premiums for the same home can vary widely between insurers. A quote-comparison service lets you see several options in one place so you can compare coverage and price side by side.','["One short form instead of contacting insurers one by one","Compare dwelling coverage, deductibles and liability limits — not just the premium","Free to use, with no obligation to buy"]','Compare Quotes Now','Free · No obligation · Takes about 5 minutes','Ways to shop for home insurance','["Best For","Cost to Use","Key Advantage"]','[{"name":"QuoteNest","tagline":"Sample quote marketplace","highlight":true,"rating":4.5,"cells":["Comparing several insurers fast","Free, no obligation","Multiple quotes from one form"],"ctaLabel":"Get quotes"},{"name":"Independent insurance agent","tagline":"Local or online","highlight":false,"rating":4.4,"cells":["Complex homes or high-value items","Free (paid by insurer commission)","Personal advice"],"ctaLabel":"Compare"},{"name":"Going direct to one insurer","tagline":"Single-company quote","highlight":false,"rating":3.8,"cells":["Bundling with existing auto policy","Free","Simple if you''re loyal to one brand"],"ctaLabel":"Compare"}]',replace('## How a home insurance quote-comparison service works\n\nA quote-comparison service collects basic details about you and your home once — address, year built, square footage, roof age, construction type and the coverage you want — and shares them with participating insurers or agents. You then receive several quotes, either on screen, by email or from licensed agents who follow up. Instead of filling in the same form five times, you fill it in once.\n\nServices like QuoteNest are typically free to use because they''re paid by the insurers and agents in their network. Your quote and the eventual premium come from the insurer, not from the comparison service.\n\n## Who it''s for\n\nComparing quotes is worth doing if:\n\n- You''re buying a home and need a policy before closing.\n- Your renewal premium has gone up noticeably.\n- You''ve made improvements — a new roof, updated wiring, a security system — that could lower your rate.\n- You haven''t shopped your policy in two or three years.\n\nIf you have an unusual property — a historic home, significant high-value items, or a home in a high-risk wildfire or coastal area — an **independent agent** who can explain specialist options may be a better first stop.\n\n## Why prices vary so much\n\nEach insurer prices risk differently. They weigh factors such as your home''s rebuild cost, location, roof age and material, claims history, chosen deductible and, in most US states, a credit-based insurance score. Because each company uses its own model, two quotes for identical coverage can be far apart. That''s exactly why comparing several quotes is worthwhile.\n\n## Compare coverage, not just the price\n\nThe cheapest quote isn''t the best deal if it leaves you underinsured. When you compare, line up these items:\n\n- **Dwelling coverage (Coverage A):** should reflect the cost to *rebuild* your home, not its market value.\n- **Replacement cost vs. actual cash value:** replacement cost pays to replace damaged items at today''s prices; actual cash value deducts depreciation.\n- **Deductible:** a higher deductible lowers your premium but increases what you pay per claim. Check whether wind or hail has a separate percentage deductible.\n- **Personal liability:** many people choose at least $300,000; consider an umbrella policy if you have significant assets.\n- **Loss of use:** pays living expenses if your home becomes uninhabitable after a covered loss.\n- **Exclusions:** standard policies generally don''t cover flood or earthquake. Flood insurance is available separately, including through the National Flood Insurance Program.\n\nOur guide to [what each part of a home insurance policy covers](/articles/home-insurance-coverage-explained) explains these in more detail.\n\n## What to expect after you submit the form\n\nDepending on the service, you may see instant quotes, receive emails, or get calls from licensed agents. Read the consent wording on the form so you know how you''ll be contacted. Have your current policy''s declarations page handy so you can compare like for like.\n\n## Tips for getting an accurate quote\n\n1. Use the same coverage limits and deductible across every quote.\n2. Have details ready: roof age, renovation dates, safety features.\n3. Ask about discounts for bundling, security systems, claims-free history and impact-resistant roofing.\n4. Check the insurer''s financial strength rating and complaint record with your state insurance department.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nShopping your home insurance every few years is one of the simplest ways to make sure you''re not overpaying. QuoteNest makes it faster to collect several quotes at once. Use it to narrow your options, then compare coverage line by line before you switch.','\n',char(10)),'["One form returns multiple quotes, saving time","Free to use with no obligation to buy","Makes it easy to benchmark your renewal price","Good starting point for first-time buyers"]','["You may be contacted by several agents — read the consent terms","Not every insurer participates in every network","Specialist or high-value homes may need an independent agent"]','If you haven''t compared home insurance in a couple of years, QuoteNest is a quick, free way to see whether you could get the same coverage for less. Compare coverage line by line before switching.',1,1,1,'Free: Home Insurance Quote Comparison Worksheet','A printable worksheet to compare quotes like for like.','2026-11-24T05:59:32.385+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.385+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpd000087ds06t4l0ole','how-to-choose-an-ai-writing-assistant','How to Choose an AI Writing Assistant for Your Business','AI writing tools all promise to save time. Here''s a practical framework for choosing one that fits your team''s workflow, protects your data and doesn''t create more editing work than it saves.','ai-saas',replace('AI writing assistants have gone from novelty to default in a remarkably short time. Most business software now ships with some form of "write it for me" button, and there are dozens of standalone tools competing for your budget. The hard part is no longer finding an AI writer — it''s choosing one that genuinely saves your team time without creating new risks.\n\nThis guide walks through the questions we use when evaluating AI writing tools for small and mid-sized businesses.\n\n## Start with the job, not the tool\n\nBefore comparing products, write down the three or four tasks you actually want help with. Common business use cases include:\n\n- **First drafts** of blog posts, landing pages and product descriptions.\n- **Email and sales outreach** — personalised follow-ups at volume.\n- **Internal documentation** — turning messy notes into readable process docs.\n- **Rewriting and editing** — adjusting tone, shortening, or translating existing copy.\n- **Summarising** long documents, reports or call transcripts.\n\nA tool that is excellent at long-form marketing drafts may be clumsy for quick email replies inside your inbox. Matching the tool to the job is the single biggest factor in whether your team keeps using it after the first month.\n\n## Evaluate output quality on your own content\n\nVendor demos are optimised to impress. Instead, run a short trial using real tasks from your backlog:\n\n1. Pick five pieces of content your team produced recently.\n2. Give each tool the same brief you gave your writer.\n3. Measure how long it takes a human to edit the AI draft into something publishable.\n\nThe metric that matters is **editing time saved**, not how impressive the first draft looks. A draft that''s 80% right but needs every fact checked can take longer than writing from scratch.\n\nPay special attention to factual accuracy. Language models can produce confident statements that are simply wrong. Any content containing statistics, prices, legal statements or product claims needs human verification before it''s published — especially in regulated areas like finance and insurance.\n\n## Check brand voice and style controls\n\nGood business tools let you define a brand voice once and apply it consistently. Look for:\n\n- Saved style guides or "brand voice" profiles.\n- The ability to upload reference documents the tool should follow.\n- Reusable templates for recurring content types.\n- Team-wide sharing of prompts and templates.\n\nWithout these, every user ends up writing their own prompts, and output quality varies widely across the team.\n\n## Take data privacy seriously\n\nThis is where business tools and consumer tools differ most. Before you paste customer data, internal plans or unpublished content into any AI tool, find clear answers to these questions:\n\n- **Is my data used to train the vendor''s models?** Many business plans exclude customer data from training by default; some consumer plans don''t.\n- **How long is data retained?** Can admins set retention periods or delete history?\n- **Where is data processed and stored?** This matters for GDPR and other regional rules.\n- **Is there a data processing agreement (DPA)?** If you handle EU or UK personal data, you''ll likely need one.\n- **What security certifications does the vendor hold?** SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001 reports are a reasonable baseline for business software.\n\nIf a vendor can''t answer these clearly, that''s your answer.\n\n## Look at admin controls and seat management\n\nAs soon as more than a handful of people use a tool, you''ll want:\n\n- **Single sign-on (SSO)** so access is tied to your identity provider.\n- **Role-based permissions** — who can create templates, who can see shared workspaces.\n- **Usage visibility** so you can see whether seats are actually being used.\n- **Centralised billing** instead of a dozen personal subscriptions on expense reports.\n\nThese features are often reserved for higher-tier plans, so factor them into your pricing comparison.\n\n## Integrations decide daily usage\n\nThe best AI writer is the one that''s already where your team works. Check whether the tool integrates with your:\n\n- Document editor (Google Docs, Microsoft Word).\n- Email client and CRM.\n- CMS (WordPress, Webflow and similar).\n- Chat tools such as Slack or Teams.\n\nA browser extension that works across sites can cover gaps, but native integrations tend to get used more.\n\n## Calculate the real cost\n\nMost AI writing tools charge per seat per month, sometimes with usage limits on top. To compare fairly:\n\n- Multiply the per-seat price by the number of people who will **actually** use it weekly.\n- Check whether usage caps (words, credits or "generations") are realistic for your volume.\n- Note which features — SSO, brand voice, API access — require a higher tier.\n- Consider whether an AI feature already included in software you pay for covers your needs.\n\nFor many teams, the cheapest path is to start with what''s bundled in their existing tools and add a dedicated writer only for the use cases where it clearly outperforms.\n\n## A simple scoring template\n\nWhen we compare tools, we score each out of five on:\n\n| Criterion | What we look for |\n| --- | --- |\n| Output quality | Editing time saved on real tasks |\n| Brand controls | Saved voice, templates, references |\n| Privacy & security | No training on your data, DPA, certifications |\n| Admin features | SSO, roles, usage reporting |\n| Integrations | Works inside your existing tools |\n| Value | Real cost per active user |\n\nWeight the criteria according to your priorities — a regulated business should weight privacy much more heavily than a solo creator.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nChoose an AI writing assistant the same way you''d hire a junior writer: test it on real work, check how much supervision it needs, and make sure it handles confidential information responsibly. If you also want help with meeting notes and follow-ups, see our comparison of [AI meeting assistants for small teams](/offers/best-ai-meeting-assistant) and our [privacy checklist for AI note-takers](/articles/ai-meeting-note-taker-privacy-checklist).','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','How to Choose an AI Writing Assistant: Business Buyer''s Guide','A practical framework for choosing an AI writing assistant: use cases, output quality, data privacy, admin controls, integrations and real total cost.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-21T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.388+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.388+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpd200097ds0wsvhb1a9','ai-meeting-note-taker-privacy-checklist','AI Meeting Note-Takers: A Privacy, Consent and Security Checklist','AI note-takers can save hours a week, but recording calls raises real consent and data questions. Use this checklist before you let a bot join your client meetings.','ai-saas',replace('AI meeting assistants are one of the most useful AI tools a business can adopt: they join calls, produce transcripts and summaries, and push action items to your CRM. They also record conversations — sometimes with clients, candidates or partners who didn''t expect to be recorded. Getting the privacy and consent side right protects your relationships and keeps you on the right side of the law.\n\nUse this checklist before rolling an AI note-taker out to your team.\n\n## 1. Understand recording consent rules\n\nRecording laws vary by location. In the United States, some states require only one party to a conversation to consent to a recording, while others require **all** parties to consent. Many other countries — and the GDPR in the EU and UK — impose their own requirements around notice, lawful basis and transparency.\n\nBecause your calls may include people in several jurisdictions, the safest practical policy is simple: **always tell participants the meeting is being recorded and transcribed, and give them a way to object.** This is general information, not legal advice; check with your legal adviser for your specific situation.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Does the tool announce itself when it joins a call?\n- Can admins enforce an automatic recording notice in calendar invites or chat?\n- Can a participant easily ask for the bot to be removed?\n\n## 2. Control which meetings get recorded\n\nAuto-join is convenient, but you rarely want a bot in every meeting. Sensitive HR conversations, legal discussions and board meetings may need to stay off the record.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Can you restrict auto-join to external meetings, or to specific calendars?\n- Can individual users exclude meetings by keyword or category?\n- Can admins block recording for certain teams entirely?\n\n## 3. Find out whether your data trains AI models\n\nThis is the question to ask every vendor. Some tools use customer recordings and transcripts to improve their models; many business plans contractually exclude your data from training.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Is customer data excluded from model training by default?\n- Is that commitment in the contract or data processing agreement, not just a blog post?\n- Which third-party AI providers process your data, and under what terms?\n\n## 4. Set data retention periods\n\nRecordings and transcripts are sensitive records. Keeping them forever increases risk without adding much value.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Can admins set automatic deletion periods for recordings and transcripts?\n- Can users delete individual recordings?\n- Can you export data if you change vendors?\n\n## 5. Lock down access\n\nMeeting notes often contain pricing, personal details and strategic plans. Make sure only the right people can see them.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Is single sign-on (SSO) supported?\n- Are notes private to attendees by default, with deliberate sharing?\n- Are shared links protected, or can anyone with the link view the recording?\n- Is there an audit log of who viewed or exported recordings?\n\n## 6. Review vendor security\n\nTreat an AI note-taker like any other vendor that handles confidential data.\n\n**Checklist:**\n\n- Does the vendor hold an independent security attestation such as SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001?\n- Is data encrypted in transit and at rest?\n- Where is data stored, and can you choose a region?\n- Is a data processing agreement available for GDPR purposes?\n\n## 7. Write a short internal policy\n\nA one-page policy prevents most problems. Cover:\n\n- Which meetings may and may not be recorded.\n- How participants are notified.\n- Who can access recordings and summaries.\n- How long data is kept.\n- Who to contact with concerns.\n\n## 8. Remember that AI summaries can be wrong\n\nSummaries are a starting point, not an official record. Speaker attribution errors and missed nuance are common. Ask the meeting owner to review the summary before sharing it externally or relying on it for decisions.\n\n## Putting it into practice\n\nThe good news: the better AI meeting tools build these controls in. When we compared options in our [AI meeting assistant comparison](/offers/best-ai-meeting-assistant), consent and retention controls were among the main differences between dedicated tools and basic built-in recaps. For a broader framework on evaluating AI software, read [how to choose an AI writing assistant](/articles/how-to-choose-an-ai-writing-assistant).','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','AI Meeting Note-Taker Privacy & Consent Checklist','Before you roll out an AI meeting note-taker, check recording consent, data retention, model training, access controls and vendor security with this checklist.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-15T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.391+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.391+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpd5000a7ds0c8s4qedp','how-credit-scores-work','How Credit Scores Work: The 5 Factors That Matter (and the Myths That Don''t)','Your credit score affects loan rates, rental applications and even insurance prices in many states. Here''s what actually moves it — and the common myths you can ignore.','finance',replace('A credit score is a three-digit number that summarises how likely you are to repay borrowed money, based on the information in your credit reports. Lenders use it to decide whether to approve you and what interest rate to offer. Landlords, utility companies and — in most US states — insurers may also use credit information.\n\nUnderstanding how scores are calculated takes most of the mystery (and stress) out of improving yours.\n\n## Credit reports vs. credit scores\n\nYour **credit report** is a record of your credit accounts and payment history, maintained by the three nationwide credit bureaus: Equifax, Experian and TransUnion. Your **credit score** is calculated from that report using a scoring model.\n\nThe two best-known scoring models are FICO and VantageScore. Both typically use a range of 300 to 850, where higher is better. Because each bureau may hold slightly different information, and lenders use different model versions, it''s normal to have several slightly different scores at once.\n\n## The five factors in a FICO score\n\nFICO publishes the general weighting of the categories in its scores:\n\n| Factor | Approximate weight | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Payment history | 35% | Do you pay on time? |\n| Amounts owed | 30% | How much of your available credit are you using? |\n| Length of credit history | 15% | How long have your accounts been open? |\n| New credit | 10% | Have you opened or applied for several accounts recently? |\n| Credit mix | 10% | Do you manage different types of credit? |\n\n### Payment history (about 35%)\n\nThis is the biggest factor. Late payments, collections and bankruptcies hurt your score, and the damage is greater when they''re recent and severe. A payment usually isn''t reported as late to the bureaus until it''s 30 days past due, but late fees from the lender can apply sooner.\n\n**What helps:** set up autopay for at least the minimum payment on every account.\n\n### Amounts owed / credit utilisation (about 30%)\n\nUtilisation is the share of your available revolving credit that you''re using. If you have a $10,000 total credit limit and a $3,000 balance, your utilisation is 30%. Lower is generally better, and people with the highest scores often keep utilisation in the single digits.\n\n**What helps:** pay down balances, pay before the statement closing date, or ask for a credit limit increase (without increasing spending).\n\n### Length of credit history (about 15%)\n\nScoring models consider the age of your oldest account, newest account and the average age of all accounts.\n\n**What helps:** keep older no-annual-fee cards open and use them occasionally.\n\n### New credit (about 10%)\n\nOpening several accounts in a short period can signal risk. Each application for credit usually results in a **hard inquiry**, which can lower your score slightly for a short time. Rate shopping for a single mortgage, auto or student loan within a short window is typically treated as one inquiry.\n\n### Credit mix (about 10%)\n\nHaving experience with both revolving credit (cards) and instalment loans (car loan, mortgage) can help slightly. It''s not worth taking on debt just to improve your mix.\n\n## Common credit score myths\n\n**"Checking my own score lowers it."** False. Checking your own credit is a soft inquiry and has no effect on your score.\n\n**"I need to carry a balance to build credit."** False. Paying your statement balance in full every month builds credit just as well — and saves you interest.\n\n**"Closing old cards always helps."** Usually the opposite. Closing a card reduces your available credit (raising utilisation) and may eventually shorten your credit history.\n\n**"My income affects my score."** Income isn''t part of your credit report, although lenders may consider it separately when approving you.\n\n**"Paying off a collection removes it."** Not necessarily. A paid collection may still appear on your report, though newer scoring models may treat paid collections more favourably.\n\n## How to check your credit for free\n\nYou can get free credit reports from all three bureaus through AnnualCreditReport.com, the official site authorised by federal law, and the bureaus now offer free weekly reports there. Many banks and card issuers also show a free score. Check your reports for accounts you don''t recognise and incorrect late payments, and dispute errors directly with the bureau.\n\n## Protecting your credit\n\nFraud can damage your score quickly. A free [credit freeze](/articles/credit-monitoring-vs-credit-freeze) at each bureau blocks most new-account fraud. If you''d like ongoing alerts across all three bureaus, compare options in our [credit monitoring comparison](/offers/credit-monitoring-service).\n\n## Bottom line\n\nPay on time, keep balances low relative to your limits, and let your accounts age. Those three habits account for the large majority of a typical score — everything else is fine-tuning.\n\n*This article is general information and not personalised financial advice.*','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','How Credit Scores Work: 5 Factors & Common Myths Explained','Learn what goes into your credit score — payment history, utilisation, credit age, new credit and mix — plus common myths and practical ways to improve it.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-19T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.393+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.393+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpd7000b7ds0pm2e80vg','credit-monitoring-vs-credit-freeze','Credit Monitoring vs. Credit Freeze: Which Do You Actually Need?','A credit freeze is free and blocks most new-account fraud. Credit monitoring alerts you to changes. Here''s how they differ, what each costs, and when it makes sense to use both.','finance',replace('After a data breach, the advice you''ll hear most often is "freeze your credit" or "sign up for credit monitoring." They sound similar, but they do very different jobs. One prevents fraud; the other detects it. Here''s how to decide what you need.\n\n## The quick comparison\n\n| | Credit freeze | Fraud alert | Credit monitoring |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| What it does | Blocks most lenders from accessing your report | Asks lenders to verify your identity | Alerts you to changes on your report |\n| Prevents or detects? | Prevents | Discourages | Detects |\n| Cost | Free by US federal law | Free | Free (limited) to monthly fee |\n| Where to set it up | Each bureau separately | One bureau (it notifies the others) | Bureau, bank or paid service |\n| Effect on your score | None | None | None |\n\n## What a credit freeze does\n\nA credit freeze (also called a security freeze) restricts access to your credit report. Because most lenders won''t open a new account without checking your credit, a freeze stops most fraudsters from opening new credit cards or loans in your name.\n\nKey facts:\n\n- **It''s free.** Since 2018, federal law requires Equifax, Experian and TransUnion to let you freeze and unfreeze your credit at no charge.\n- **You need to freeze at all three bureaus** for full protection.\n- **It doesn''t affect your score** or your existing accounts.\n- **You can lift it temporarily** when you apply for credit — online or by phone, usually quickly.\n\nWhat a freeze doesn''t do: it won''t stop misuse of your **existing** accounts, and it won''t stop fraud that doesn''t involve a credit check, such as some tax or medical identity theft.\n\n## What a fraud alert does\n\nA fraud alert tells lenders to take extra steps to verify your identity before opening an account. You place it with one bureau, which must notify the other two. An initial alert lasts one year; victims of identity theft can get an extended alert lasting seven years. It''s free, but it''s a request to lenders rather than a hard block.\n\n## What credit monitoring does\n\nCredit monitoring watches your credit reports and notifies you when something changes — a new account, a hard inquiry, a change of address or a new collection. It''s a **detection** tool: it tells you something happened so you can act quickly.\n\nMonitoring comes in different forms:\n\n- **Free single-bureau monitoring** from some banks, card issuers and the bureaus themselves.\n- **Paid three-bureau monitoring** services, which typically add identity restoration support and an identity theft insurance policy covering certain expenses.\n\n## When monitoring is worth paying for\n\nPaid monitoring can make sense when:\n\n- Your data has been exposed in a breach and you want ongoing three-bureau alerts.\n- You''re about to make a major application (like a mortgage) and want to catch errors early.\n- You want specialist help with the recovery process if your identity is stolen.\n- You prefer the convenience of one dashboard rather than managing three bureau accounts.\n\nRead the terms carefully: check the monthly price after any trial, cancellation process, and the exact limits and exclusions of any insurance coverage.\n\n## Our recommended setup\n\nFor most people:\n\n1. **Freeze your credit at all three bureaus.** It''s free, and it''s the strongest protection against new-account fraud.\n2. **Check your free credit reports regularly** at AnnualCreditReport.com.\n3. **Turn on free alerts** from your bank or card issuer.\n4. **Add paid three-bureau monitoring** if you want hands-off alerts and restoration support — compare options in our [credit monitoring comparison](/offers/credit-monitoring-service).\n\n## If you think you''re a victim of identity theft\n\nAct quickly:\n\n- Contact the company where the fraud occurred and close or freeze the affected accounts.\n- Place a fraud alert and freeze your credit.\n- Report it at IdentityTheft.gov, the US Federal Trade Commission''s official resource, which creates a recovery plan.\n- Review your credit reports and dispute fraudulent items.\n\nFor more on how your score is calculated, read [how credit scores work](/articles/how-credit-scores-work).\n\n*This article is general information and not personalised financial or legal advice.*','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','Credit Monitoring vs Credit Freeze: Differences, Costs & When to Use Each','Compare credit freezes, fraud alerts and credit monitoring. Learn what each does, what it costs, and how to combine them to protect your identity.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-12T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.395+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.395+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpd9000c7ds0dtwid7w8','home-insurance-coverage-explained','Home Insurance Coverage Explained: What Each Part of Your Policy Covers','Dwelling, other structures, personal property, loss of use, liability and medical payments: a plain-English guide to the six core coverages in a standard homeowners policy.','home-insurance',replace('A homeowners insurance policy can run to dozens of pages, but most of what matters sits on a single page: the **declarations page**. It lists your coverage types, limits and deductibles. Once you understand the six core coverages, you can compare quotes with confidence and spot gaps before you need to make a claim.\n\nThis guide covers the most common US policy type, often called an **HO-3**. Details vary by insurer and state, so always read your own policy.\n\n## The six core coverages\n\n### Coverage A — Dwelling\n\nPays to repair or rebuild the structure of your home — walls, roof, built-in appliances, plumbing and electrical systems — after a covered loss such as fire or wind.\n\n**Key point:** your dwelling limit should reflect the cost to **rebuild** your home at today''s construction prices, not its market value or what you paid for it. Land doesn''t need to be insured, and rebuild costs can be higher or lower than market value.\n\n### Coverage B — Other structures\n\nCovers structures not attached to your house, such as a detached garage, shed or fence. It''s commonly set at a percentage of your dwelling coverage (often around 10%).\n\n### Coverage C — Personal property\n\nCovers your belongings — furniture, clothing, electronics — whether they''re at home or, in many policies, while you''re travelling. It''s often set at 50–70% of dwelling coverage.\n\nMany policies have **sub-limits** for certain categories such as jewellery, watches, firearms and collectibles. If you own valuable items, ask about scheduling them separately (sometimes called a rider or floater).\n\n### Coverage D — Loss of use\n\nPays additional living expenses — such as a hotel and higher food costs — if a covered loss makes your home uninhabitable while it''s being repaired.\n\n### Coverage E — Personal liability\n\nCovers you if you''re legally responsible for injuring someone or damaging their property, including legal defence costs. Many people choose at least $300,000 in liability coverage. If you have significant assets, consider an **umbrella policy** for additional protection.\n\n### Coverage F — Medical payments to others\n\nPays small medical bills for guests injured on your property, regardless of fault. Limits are usually modest.\n\n## Replacement cost vs. actual cash value\n\nThis is one of the most important — and most overlooked — details when you compare quotes.\n\n- **Replacement cost** pays to repair or replace damaged property with new items of similar kind and quality, without deducting for depreciation.\n- **Actual cash value (ACV)** pays replacement cost minus depreciation. A ten-year-old sofa might be worth very little under ACV.\n\nSome policies cover the dwelling at replacement cost but personal property at ACV unless you add a replacement cost endorsement. Check both. You may also see **extended** or **guaranteed replacement cost** options that pay above your dwelling limit if rebuild costs surge after a disaster.\n\n## Deductibles\n\nYour deductible is the amount you pay toward a covered claim before insurance pays. A higher deductible lowers your premium but means more out of pocket per claim.\n\nWatch for **percentage deductibles** for wind, hail or hurricanes in some areas. A 2% wind deductible on a home with $400,000 of dwelling coverage means you''d pay the first $8,000 of a wind claim.\n\n## What standard policies usually exclude\n\nStandard homeowners policies typically **do not** cover:\n\n- **Flood damage.** Flood insurance is sold separately, including through the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) and private insurers. Many policies have a waiting period before coverage starts.\n- **Earthquakes.** Usually requires a separate policy or endorsement.\n- **Wear and tear, maintenance issues and pests.**\n- **Sewer or drain backup** — often available as an inexpensive add-on endorsement.\n\n## Using this when you compare quotes\n\nWhen you gather quotes, line them up using the same:\n\n1. Dwelling limit (based on rebuild cost).\n2. Personal property limit and valuation (replacement cost or ACV).\n3. Liability limit.\n4. Deductibles, including any wind or hail deductible.\n5. Endorsements such as water backup or scheduled valuables.\n\nOur guide to [comparing home insurance quotes without overpaying](/articles/how-to-compare-home-insurance-quotes) walks through the process step by step, or you can [compare quotes from multiple insurers](/offers/compare-home-insurance-quotes) in one place.\n\n*This article is general information, not insurance advice. Coverage depends on your policy wording and state regulations.*','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','Home Insurance Coverage Explained: Coverages A–F in Plain English','Understand the six main parts of a homeowners insurance policy, replacement cost vs actual cash value, deductibles, and what standard policies usually exclude.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-20T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.398+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.398+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdb000d7ds0s9rq0u8h','how-to-compare-home-insurance-quotes','How to Compare Home Insurance Quotes Without Overpaying','The cheapest quote isn''t always the best deal. A step-by-step process for gathering quotes, comparing coverage like for like, and finding legitimate discounts.','home-insurance',replace('Home insurance premiums for the same house can vary significantly between insurers, because each company prices risk using its own model. That''s good news for shoppers: comparing quotes every couple of years — or whenever your renewal jumps — is one of the easiest ways to keep costs in check.\n\nThe catch is that quotes are only comparable if the coverage matches. Here''s a step-by-step process.\n\n## Step 1: Gather your details\n\nHaving this information ready makes quotes faster and more accurate:\n\n- Your current policy''s **declarations page** (limits, deductibles and endorsements).\n- Home details: year built, square footage, construction type, number of storeys.\n- **Roof age and material** — one of the biggest pricing factors.\n- Dates of major updates to plumbing, electrical and HVAC systems.\n- Safety features: monitored alarm, smoke detectors, sprinklers, deadbolts.\n- Your claims history for the past five years.\n\n## Step 2: Decide on the coverage you need\n\nBefore you compare prices, decide what you''re comparing. If you''re unsure what each coverage does, read [home insurance coverage explained](/articles/home-insurance-coverage-explained). At minimum, decide:\n\n- **Dwelling limit** based on the cost to rebuild your home.\n- **Personal property limit**, and whether you want replacement cost rather than actual cash value.\n- **Liability limit** — many people choose $300,000 or more.\n- **Deductible** you''re comfortable paying out of pocket.\n- **Endorsements** you need, such as water backup or scheduled jewellery.\n\n## Step 3: Collect at least three quotes\n\nYou have three main ways to shop:\n\n| Method | Pros | Cons |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Quote-comparison service | Several quotes from one form; fast | You may be contacted by several agents |\n| Independent agent | Personal advice; access to multiple insurers | Coverage of the market varies by agency |\n| Direct from an insurer | Simple, especially if bundling auto | Only one company''s price |\n\nA mix works well: use a [quote-comparison service](/offers/compare-home-insurance-quotes) to benchmark the market, then ask an independent agent or your current insurer to match or beat the best option.\n\n## Step 4: Compare coverage line by line\n\nPut the quotes side by side and check that each one includes the same:\n\n1. Dwelling, other structures, personal property and loss-of-use limits.\n2. Liability and medical payments limits.\n3. All-perils deductible **and** any separate wind/hail or hurricane deductible.\n4. Valuation method for the dwelling and belongings.\n5. Endorsements and sub-limits.\n\nIf one quote is dramatically cheaper, look for what''s missing — a lower dwelling limit, actual cash value on the roof, or a high percentage wind deductible are common culprits.\n\n## Step 5: Evaluate the insurer, not just the price\n\nA low premium isn''t much use if claims are difficult. Check:\n\n- **Financial strength ratings** from independent rating agencies such as AM Best.\n- **Complaint data** published by your state insurance department or the National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC).\n- **Claims process** — online claims, 24/7 support and local adjusters.\n\n## Step 6: Ask about discounts\n\nCommon discounts include:\n\n- **Bundling** home and auto insurance with one company.\n- **Protective devices** such as monitored alarms and water leak sensors.\n- **Claims-free** history.\n- **New or impact-resistant roof.**\n- **Paying annually** instead of monthly, or enrolling in paperless billing and autopay.\n\nRaising your deductible also lowers your premium — just make sure you could comfortably pay it.\n\n## Step 7: Switch without a coverage gap\n\nIf you decide to switch:\n\n1. Start the new policy **before** cancelling the old one, ideally on the same date.\n2. If you have a mortgage, tell your lender — premiums are often paid through escrow, and the lender must be listed on the new policy.\n3. Cancel the old policy in writing and ask about any refund of unused premium.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nGather accurate details, match coverage across every quote, check the insurer''s reputation, and ask for discounts. Doing this every couple of years — or whenever your renewal price jumps — keeps you from quietly overpaying.\n\n*This article is general information, not insurance advice.*','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','How to Compare Home Insurance Quotes: Step-by-Step Guide','Gather and compare home insurance quotes the right way: prepare your details, match coverage, evaluate insurers, find discounts and switch without a gap.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-14T05:59:32.327+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.400+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.400+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdd000e7ds03qt67t06','do-you-need-a-vpn','Do You Really Need a VPN? When It Helps — and When It Doesn''t','VPN ads promise total privacy and security. The truth is more useful: a VPN solves some specific problems very well and does nothing for others. Here''s how to tell which situation you''re in.','ai-saas',replace('If you''ve watched a video online recently, you''ve probably seen a VPN advert promising to make you "completely anonymous" or "safe from hackers". Those claims are exaggerated. A VPN is a genuinely useful tool, but only for certain jobs. Understanding what it does will help you decide whether you need one, and stop you paying for protection you think you have but don''t.\n\n## What a VPN actually does\n\nA VPN (virtual private network) creates an encrypted connection, often called a tunnel, between your device and a server run by the VPN provider. Your internet traffic travels through that tunnel before going out to the wider internet.\n\nThat has two direct effects:\n\n1. **People on your local network and your internet provider can''t see what you''re doing.** They can see that you''re connected to a VPN and how much data you use, but not which websites you visit or what you send.\n2. **Websites see the VPN server''s IP address instead of yours.** That hides your approximate location from the sites you visit and makes it look as if you''re browsing from the VPN server''s country.\n\nThat''s essentially it. Everything a VPN offers flows from those two effects.\n\n## When a VPN genuinely helps\n\n### Public Wi-Fi\n\nCafés, airports, hotels and co-working spaces often run open or shared networks. Most websites already use HTTPS encryption, which protects the content of your connection, but a VPN adds a strong extra layer. It also hides *which* sites and services you use from anyone else on that network, and protects apps that don''t encrypt their traffic properly. If you regularly work from public networks, this is one of the best reasons to use a VPN.\n\n### Keeping your browsing private from your internet provider\n\nWithout a VPN, your internet service provider (ISP) can see the domain names you visit. In some countries ISPs may log or use this information. A VPN moves that visibility from your ISP to the VPN provider, which is why choosing a trustworthy VPN matters so much (see our guide to [choosing a VPN](/articles/how-to-choose-a-vpn)).\n\n### Travelling abroad\n\nWhen you travel, some services such as banking apps, news sites or work tools may behave differently or block access from unfamiliar countries. Connecting to a server in your home country can help you use them normally. Check the service''s own terms: some streaming services restrict VPN use, and some banks may still ask you to verify unusual logins.\n\n### Remote work and sensitive tasks\n\nFreelancers and small teams often handle client files and logins from many locations. A VPN is a sensible baseline for protecting that traffic on networks you don''t control. (Many companies also run their own business VPN for accessing internal systems. That''s a different product from a consumer VPN.)\n\n### Reducing price and ad tracking by location\n\nBecause websites see the VPN server''s location, a VPN can reduce location-based tracking and personalisation. It won''t stop tracking through cookies or accounts, though (more on that below).\n\n## What a VPN does *not* do\n\nThis is where the adverts usually overstate things.\n\n- **It doesn''t make you anonymous.** If you log in to Google, Facebook or Amazon, they know it''s you, VPN or not. Cookies, browser fingerprinting and account logins all identify you regardless of your IP address.\n- **It doesn''t protect you from malware or phishing.** A VPN won''t stop you downloading a malicious file or typing your password into a fake website. Some VPN apps include optional malicious-site or ad blocking, which helps a little, but it''s no substitute for good security habits and up-to-date software.\n- **It doesn''t hide your activity from the VPN provider.** You are shifting trust from your internet provider to the VPN company. If the provider keeps logs, your activity is recorded there instead.\n- **It doesn''t make illegal activity legal.** Laws still apply, and VPN use itself is restricted in some countries.\n- **It won''t fix a slow connection.** A VPN usually makes your connection slightly slower, because traffic takes a longer route. Good providers keep this difference small.\n\n## A quick self-check: do you need one?\n\nAnswer these five questions:\n\n1. Do you often use public or shared Wi-Fi (cafés, hotels, airports, offices)?\n2. Do you travel abroad and need your usual apps and services to work normally?\n3. Would you prefer your internet provider not to see which sites you visit?\n4. Do you handle client or work data on networks you don''t control?\n5. Do you want to reduce location-based tracking when you browse?\n\n**If you answered "yes" to two or more,** a reputable paid VPN is likely worth the cost, which is usually a few dollars a month on a longer plan.\n\n**If you answered "no" to all of them,** you probably don''t need one right now. Spend your effort on the security basics instead.\n\n## The security basics matter more\n\nA VPN is one layer of protection. These protect you far more for most people:\n\n- **Use a password manager** and a unique password for every account.\n- **Turn on two-factor authentication** for email, banking and social media.\n- **Keep your devices and apps updated.**\n- **Be suspicious of unexpected links and attachments**, even from people you know.\n\n## Free VPNs: proceed with caution\n\nRunning a VPN service costs real money for servers and bandwidth. If a VPN is completely free, ask how it pays for itself. Some free VPNs are limited "taster" versions of reputable paid services, which is fine. Others have been found by researchers to show intrusive ads, collect and share browsing data, or offer weak security. For anything sensitive, a reputable paid service with a clear no-logs policy is the safer choice.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nA VPN is excellent at protecting your connection on networks you don''t trust, hiding your browsing from your internet provider, and helping when you travel. It is not an invisibility cloak, an antivirus or a fix for weak passwords. If those first benefits match how you use the internet, it''s a worthwhile and inexpensive upgrade.\n\nNext, read our checklist on [how to choose a VPN](/articles/how-to-choose-a-vpn), and our guide to [setting one up on all your devices](/articles/how-to-set-up-a-vpn).','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','Do You Really Need a VPN? An Honest Guide','What a VPN actually protects, what it can''t do, and the everyday situations where it''s worth paying for one. A plain-English guide without the hype.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-24T05:59:32.319+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.402+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.402+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdg000f7ds0n7kqklrs','how-to-choose-a-vpn','How to Choose a VPN: 10 Things to Check Before You Pay','Most VPNs look identical on their sales pages. These ten checks separate trustworthy services from the rest: from independent no-logs audits to renewal pricing and refund terms.','ai-saas',replace('Every VPN website promises "military-grade encryption", "blazing speeds" and "total privacy". Because the marketing is so similar, it''s hard to tell services apart. The good news is that the differences that actually matter can be checked. Work through these ten points before you subscribe.\n\nIf you''re still deciding whether you need a VPN at all, start with [do you really need a VPN?](/articles/do-you-need-a-vpn)\n\n## 1. A no-logs policy that has been independently audited\n\nWhen you use a VPN, the provider can technically see the same things your internet provider could. That''s why a **no-logs policy** (a commitment not to record your browsing activity) is the single most important feature.\n\nWords are cheap, so look for evidence:\n\n- **An independent audit** of the no-logs policy by a recognised security or accounting firm, ideally repeated over time.\n- **A clear, specific privacy policy** that says exactly what is and isn''t collected. Collecting minimal data for billing and abuse prevention is normal. Logging the sites you visit is not.\n- **A track record.** Some providers have had their no-logs claims tested in real-world situations, such as server seizures or court requests, that found no user data to hand over.\n\n## 2. Where the company is based (jurisdiction)\n\nA VPN provider must follow the laws of the country where it is legally based. Some countries have stronger privacy protections than others, and some can require companies to retain or hand over data. Jurisdiction matters less if a provider genuinely keeps no logs, but it''s still worth knowing who you''re trusting. Check the company''s registered location in its terms of service, not just its marketing.\n\n## 3. Modern, secure protocols\n\nThe protocol is the method your device uses to build the encrypted tunnel. Look for:\n\n- **WireGuard**, or the provider''s own protocol built on WireGuard: fast, modern and efficient, and usually the best default.\n- **OpenVPN**: older and slightly slower, but very well tested and widely supported.\n- **IKEv2/IPsec**: good on mobile devices because it reconnects quickly when you switch networks.\n\nAvoid services that still rely mainly on outdated protocols such as PPTP.\n\n## 4. A kill switch\n\nIf your VPN connection drops for a moment, your device may silently fall back to your normal connection and expose your traffic. A **kill switch** blocks internet access until the VPN reconnects. Check that it''s available on every platform you use, and whether it''s on by default.\n\n## 5. DNS and IP leak protection\n\nEven with a VPN connected, poorly configured apps can "leak" information, such as the domain names you look up (DNS requests) or your real IP address. Good VPN apps route DNS through their own servers and handle IPv6 and browser features like WebRTC properly. After you install a VPN, test for leaks (our [setup guide](/articles/how-to-set-up-a-vpn) explains how).\n\n## 6. Real-world speed\n\nAll VPNs slow your connection somewhat. Good ones keep the difference small enough that you won''t notice it for browsing, video calls or streaming. Speed depends heavily on the server''s distance from you and how busy it is, so:\n\n- Use the free trial or money-back period to **test speeds yourself**, at the times of day you normally use the internet.\n- Test a nearby server and a server in any country you''ll need while travelling.\n\n## 7. Server locations you actually need\n\nA huge server count is mostly marketing. What matters is that the provider has **reliable servers in the countries you care about**: usually your home country plus anywhere you travel or work.\n\n## 8. Apps for all your devices, and enough connections\n\nCheck for well-reviewed apps on the devices you own: Windows, macOS, iOS, Android and, if needed, smart TVs, browser extensions or routers. Also check the **simultaneous connection limit**. Some plans cover a set number of devices, while others allow unlimited devices. A family typically needs more than five.\n\n## 9. Honest pricing, including the renewal price\n\nVPN pricing often looks like this: a large discount for a long first term, followed by a higher price when the plan **renews**. Before you pay:\n\n- Find the **renewal price** in the checkout page or terms.\n- Note when the plan renews, and set a calendar reminder a week before.\n- Check whether auto-renewal can be switched off easily in your account.\n\nLonger plans usually offer the best value, but only commit once you''re happy with the service.\n\n## 10. A real money-back guarantee and responsive support\n\nMost reputable VPNs offer a money-back guarantee, commonly around 30 days. Read the conditions: some limit refunds by payment method or usage. Test the support too. Ask a question through live chat during your trial and see how quickly and clearly you get an answer.\n\n## Bonus: extras that may or may not matter\n\nMany VPNs bundle additional features. Some are useful; others just pad the feature list:\n\n- **Ad and malicious-site blocking:** handy, but not a replacement for good security habits.\n- **Split tunnelling:** lets you choose which apps use the VPN. Useful if, say, a banking app dislikes VPN connections.\n- **Multi-hop (double VPN):** routes traffic through two servers for extra privacy, at the cost of speed. Most people don''t need it.\n- **Dedicated IP:** an optional add-on, useful if a work system only allows certain IP addresses.\n\n## A simple scoring sheet\n\n| Check | What "good" looks like |\n| --- | --- |\n| No-logs | Independently audited, clear policy |\n| Jurisdiction | Known, privacy-respecting location |\n| Protocols | WireGuard-based and/or OpenVPN |\n| Kill switch | Available on all your devices |\n| Leak protection | Passes DNS/IP leak tests |\n| Speed | Small slowdown in your own tests |\n| Locations | Reliable servers where you need them |\n| Devices | Apps for everything you own; enough connections |\n| Pricing | Renewal price clearly shown |\n| Refunds & support | Clear guarantee; helpful live chat |\n\n## Bottom line\n\nChoose a VPN on evidence, not adjectives: an audited no-logs policy, modern protocols, a kill switch, clean leak tests and transparent pricing. Then use the money-back period to test it on your own devices and connection. If it passes, you''ve found a good one.\n\nReady to set it up? Follow our step-by-step guide to [setting up a VPN on your phone, laptop and router](/articles/how-to-set-up-a-vpn).','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','How to Choose a VPN: 10-Point Buyer''s Checklist','Compare VPNs like an expert: audited no-logs policies, protocols, kill switch, leak protection, speed, device limits, renewal pricing and refunds.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-23T05:59:32.319+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.404+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.404+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdi000g7ds0fi5tklu8','how-to-set-up-a-vpn','How to Set Up a VPN on Your Phone, Laptop and Home Router','A step-by-step guide to installing a VPN on every device you own, the settings worth changing on day one, and a two-minute test to confirm it''s actually protecting you.','ai-saas',replace('Setting up a modern VPN takes about five minutes per device. This guide walks through each platform, the settings to change straight away, and how to check that your VPN is genuinely working. Haven''t picked a provider yet? See our checklist on [how to choose a VPN](/articles/how-to-choose-a-vpn).\n\n## Before you start\n\n- **Create your account on the VPN provider''s official website.** Avoid downloading VPN apps from links in ads or pop-ups.\n- **Download apps only from official sources:** the provider''s website, the Apple App Store, Google Play or the Microsoft Store.\n- **Use a strong, unique password** for your VPN account, and turn on two-factor authentication if it''s offered.\n\n## Windows and Mac laptops\n\n1. Download the app from the provider''s website (or the Mac App Store / Microsoft Store).\n2. Install it and sign in.\n3. Allow the app to add a VPN configuration when your system asks. This is normal.\n4. Click **Connect**. The app usually picks the fastest nearby server automatically.\n\n**Settings to turn on in the app''s preferences:**\n\n- **Kill switch:** blocks the internet if the VPN drops, so nothing leaks.\n- **Launch at startup / auto-connect:** so protection starts without you remembering.\n- **Auto-connect on untrusted Wi-Fi**, if available: connects automatically on networks you haven''t marked as trusted.\n- **Protocol: Automatic or WireGuard-based:** usually the best balance of speed and security.\n\n## iPhone and iPad\n\n1. Install the provider''s app from the App Store and sign in.\n2. Tap **Connect**, then tap **Allow** when iOS asks to add VPN configurations, and confirm with Face ID or your passcode.\n3. A **VPN** icon appears at the top of the screen when you''re connected.\n\n**Recommended:** in the app''s settings, enable **auto-connect** (sometimes called "Connect on demand") so the VPN switches on automatically on Wi-Fi networks you haven''t marked as trusted.\n\n## Android phones and tablets\n\n1. Install the provider''s app from Google Play and sign in.\n2. Tap **Connect**, then **OK** when Android asks to set up a VPN connection.\n3. A key icon appears in the status bar while connected.\n\n**Recommended:** Android has built-in settings for an **always-on VPN** and for **blocking connections without VPN**. On most phones they''re under **Settings → Network & internet → VPN → (gear icon next to your VPN app)**. The exact menu names vary by manufacturer. Turning both on gives you system-level kill switch protection.\n\n## Smart TVs and streaming devices\n\nMany VPN providers offer apps for Android TV, Google TV and Amazon Fire TV devices. Install them from the device''s app store just like on a phone. For devices that can''t run VPN apps, such as many game consoles and some smart TVs, the router option below covers them.\n\n## Your home router (covers every device at once)\n\nPutting the VPN on your router protects every device on your Wi-Fi, including ones that can''t run VPN apps. It''s more technical, so consider it optional.\n\n**First, check compatibility.** Many routers supplied by internet providers **don''t** support VPN client connections. Look in your router''s settings for a "VPN client" option with OpenVPN or WireGuard. If there isn''t one, you may need a VPN-compatible router.\n\n**General steps (they vary by router):**\n\n1. In your VPN account on the provider''s website, find **manual setup** or **router configuration** and download a configuration file (OpenVPN or WireGuard) for the server you want.\n2. Log in to your router''s admin page (the address is usually printed on the router''s label).\n3. Find **VPN client**, upload the configuration file, and enter the credentials your provider gives you for manual connections. These are often different from your normal login.\n4. Save and connect, then check the router''s status page shows the VPN as connected.\n\n**Things to know:** everything at home will appear to be in the VPN server''s location, the router''s processor may limit your top speed, and you can''t easily switch servers from your phone. Many people use apps on personal devices and reserve the router setup for TVs and consoles.\n\n## Test that your VPN is working (2 minutes)\n\nDo this once on each device after setup:\n\n1. **Disconnect the VPN** and visit an IP-checking website. Search for "what is my IP" and note your IP address and location.\n2. **Connect the VPN** and refresh the page. Your IP address and location should now match the VPN server, not your real ones.\n3. **Run a DNS leak test.** Search for "DNS leak test" and use a well-known testing site. The DNS servers listed should belong to your VPN provider, not your internet provider.\n4. **Check WebRTC** on the same kind of testing site, especially if you use the VPN mainly through a browser. Your real IP shouldn''t appear.\n\nIf your real IP or your internet provider''s DNS servers appear while connected, turn on the app''s leak protection settings, switch protocol, or contact the provider''s support.\n\n## Everyday tips\n\n- **Pick a nearby server for speed**, and a server in a specific country only when you need that location.\n- **If a website or app blocks you,** try a different server in the same country, or use split tunnelling to let that app bypass the VPN.\n- **Banking apps sometimes flag VPN logins.** That''s normal fraud protection, so verify the login or temporarily exclude the app.\n- **Respect the rules:** some services restrict VPN use in their terms, and VPN use is limited by law in a few countries.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nInstall the official app on each device, turn on the kill switch and auto-connect, and run a quick leak test. That''s all it takes to have reliable everyday protection, especially on public Wi-Fi. For the bigger picture on what a VPN can and can''t do, read [do you really need a VPN?](/articles/do-you-need-a-vpn)','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','How to Set Up a VPN: Phone, Laptop & Router Guide','Install and configure a VPN on Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android and your home router. Includes the settings to enable first and how to test for leaks.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-22T05:59:32.319+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.406+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.406+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdk000h7ds0auae4vvm','ai-tools-for-small-business','AI Tools for Small Businesses: Where They Actually Save Time','Not every AI tool is worth the subscription. These are the six areas where small businesses consistently save real hours, plus a simple 30-day plan to test AI without wasting money.','ai-saas',replace('AI tools are everywhere, and almost every software company now advertises "AI-powered" features. For a small business with limited time and budget, the question isn''t whether AI is impressive. It''s **where it reliably saves hours each week**, and how to adopt it without wasting money or creating new risks.\n\nBased on how small teams actually work, here are the six areas where AI tools most consistently pay for themselves.\n\n## 1. Writing and editing\n\n**What it does:** drafts emails, blog posts, product descriptions, social posts and proposals; rewrites text in a different tone; shortens long documents; fixes grammar.\n\n**Where it saves time:** first drafts and repetitive writing. A task that took 45 minutes can become 10 minutes of editing.\n\n**Watch out for:** confident factual mistakes, generic-sounding copy and inconsistent brand voice. Always have a person check facts, figures and claims before publishing, especially in regulated areas like finance or health.\n\n**How to choose:** see our detailed guide on [how to choose an AI writing assistant](/articles/how-to-choose-an-ai-writing-assistant).\n\n## 2. Meetings and calls\n\n**What it does:** joins video calls, produces transcripts, summarises decisions and action items, and can send notes to your CRM or project tool.\n\n**Where it saves time:** anyone who runs several client or sales calls a day. No more writing up notes or chasing "what did we agree?"\n\n**Watch out for:** recording consent, and where recordings are stored. Read our [privacy checklist for AI note-takers](/articles/ai-meeting-note-taker-privacy-checklist) before rolling one out.\n\n## 3. Customer support\n\n**What it does:** suggests replies to customer emails and chats, powers website chat assistants that answer common questions from your own help content, and categorises incoming requests.\n\n**Where it saves time:** businesses that answer the same questions repeatedly about shipping, pricing, bookings and returns.\n\n**Watch out for:** AI giving wrong answers to customers. Start with AI-*suggested* replies that a person approves, and only automate answers to simple, well-documented questions. Always offer an easy way to reach a human.\n\n## 4. Images and design\n\n**What it does:** generates images and illustrations, removes backgrounds, resizes graphics for different social platforms, and creates simple ad variations.\n\n**Where it saves time:** social media content, blog images, simple ads and mock-ups, without hiring a designer for every small job.\n\n**Watch out for:** licensing and usage rights (check the tool''s terms for commercial use), images of real people or brands, and the "AI look" that customers increasingly recognise. For your logo and core brand identity, a professional designer is still worth it.\n\n## 5. Spreadsheets, data and research\n\n**What it does:** explains spreadsheet formulas, writes them for you, summarises data, drafts reports from figures, and condenses long documents or research.\n\n**Where it saves time:** owners and managers who aren''t spreadsheet experts but need answers from their numbers.\n\n**Watch out for:** calculation errors and misread data. Verify important numbers before acting on them, and be careful about uploading confidential financial or customer data to tools that don''t protect it.\n\n## 6. Workflow automation\n\n**What it does:** connects your apps so routine tasks happen automatically. For example, when a form is submitted, add the lead to your CRM, send a welcome email and notify your team. AI features can now set up these workflows from a plain-English description.\n\n**Where it saves time:** small, repetitive admin tasks that eat up a few minutes many times a day.\n\n**Watch out for:** automations that fail silently. Check them regularly, and start with low-risk tasks.\n\n## A 30-day plan to adopt AI without wasting money\n\n**Week 1: pick one problem.** List the tasks that eat the most time each week. Choose one where mistakes are low-risk and easy to spot, such as first drafts of social posts or meeting notes.\n\n**Week 2: test two or three tools on free plans or trials.** Use real work, not demo examples. Track how long the task takes with and without AI.\n\n**Week 3: check the risks.** Read each tool''s privacy policy. Does it use your data to train its models? Can you delete your data? Is there a business plan with stronger protections? (Our guide to [free vs paid AI tools](/articles/free-vs-paid-ai-tools) covers what you typically get when you upgrade.)\n\n**Week 4: decide with numbers.** If a tool saves several hours a month and costs less than that time is worth, keep it. If not, cancel before the trial converts. Then pick the next problem.\n\n## Rules worth setting for your team\n\n- **No confidential customer data** in tools that aren''t approved for it.\n- **A human checks anything customer-facing** before it''s sent or published.\n- **Keep a list of the AI tools in use** and who has access, so subscriptions don''t multiply unnoticed.\n- **Be transparent** with customers where AI plays a significant role, for example in support chat.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe best AI investment for a small business isn''t the flashiest tool. It''s the one that removes a specific, repetitive task you do every week. Start small, measure the time saved, protect your data, and expand only when the numbers make sense.','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','AI Tools for Small Business: 6 Areas That Save Real Time','Where AI genuinely helps small businesses (writing, meetings, support, design, data and automation), how to pilot tools in 30 days, and the risks to manage.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-23T05:59:32.319+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.409+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.409+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdm000i7ds0zj3sulkx','free-vs-paid-ai-tools','Free vs Paid AI Tools: What You Actually Get When You Upgrade','Free AI plans are surprisingly capable, so when is it worth paying? We break down the real differences in limits, models, privacy and team features, and how to decide.','ai-saas',replace('Most popular AI tools, including chat assistants, writing tools, image generators and meeting note-takers, offer a free plan. Many people use them happily without ever paying. Others upgrade and find the paid version transforms how they work. So what actually changes when you pay, and how do you know if it''s worth it for you?\n\n## What free plans usually include\n\nFree plans are designed to let you experience the product and to encourage upgrades. Typically you get:\n\n- **Access to capable, though not always the most advanced, AI models.**\n- **Daily or monthly usage limits:** a cap on messages, words, images, minutes of transcription or "credits".\n- **Core features**, but often without integrations, file uploads at scale or advanced options.\n- **Standard terms on data use.** Read these carefully (see the privacy section below).\n\nFor occasional personal use (drafting the odd email, brainstorming, summarising an article) a free plan is often all you need.\n\n## What paid plans typically add\n\n### Higher limits and fewer interruptions\n\nThe most common reason people upgrade is simply **running out**. Paid plans raise or remove usage caps and often give priority access when services are busy, so you aren''t cut off in the middle of a task.\n\n### More capable models and features\n\nPaid tiers frequently unlock the provider''s most advanced models, which are better at complex reasoning, long documents and nuanced writing. They also add features such as longer context (working with bigger files), file and data analysis, image generation, voice features or custom assistants.\n\n### Privacy and data controls\n\nThis is the difference that matters most for businesses. Depending on the provider and plan:\n\n- Data from **some free or individual plans may be used to improve the provider''s models**, sometimes by default, sometimes with an opt-out setting.\n- **Business and team plans commonly exclude your data from training by default** and offer contractual commitments, data processing agreements and admin-controlled retention.\n\nAlways check the specific plan''s terms. Never assume.\n\n### Team and business features\n\nTeam plans add the features organisations need: central billing, user management, single sign-on (SSO), shared workspaces and templates, usage reports, and admin controls over data.\n\n### Integrations and automation\n\nConnecting AI to the tools you already use (email, documents, CRM, project management) or accessing it through an API is often limited to paid plans. Integrations are where a lot of the real time saving happens.\n\n## The hidden costs to watch for\n\n- **Per-seat pricing adds up.** A modest monthly price multiplied by every team member becomes significant. Only buy seats for people who will use the tool weekly.\n- **Credit systems.** Some tools sell credits that run out faster than expected for heavy tasks like long videos or high-resolution images.\n- **Overlapping subscriptions.** Many apps you already pay for now include AI features. Check before adding another subscription that does the same thing.\n- **Auto-renewing trials.** Note when a trial converts to a paid plan and set a reminder.\n\n## A simple framework: should you upgrade?\n\nAsk these four questions:\n\n1. **Do you hit the free limits at least weekly?** If yes, the paid plan removes real friction.\n2. **Does the paid version save you at least an hour a month?** Value that hour honestly. For most professionals, an hour is worth more than a typical AI subscription.\n3. **Will you use it with work or client data?** If yes, a business-grade plan with clear data protections is the responsible choice, not an optional extra.\n4. **Do you need it to work with your other tools?** If integrations matter, they usually require a paid plan.\n\n**Two or more "yes" answers** usually means upgrading is worthwhile. **All "no"** means stay on the free plan and revisit in a few months.\n\n## Getting the most from a paid plan\n\n- **Start with monthly billing** until you''re sure, then switch to annual for the discount.\n- **Use it daily for one specific task** for two weeks. Tools that become habits are the ones that pay off.\n- **Review usage every quarter** and cancel what you don''t use.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nFree AI plans are genuinely useful for occasional, non-sensitive tasks. Paying makes sense when you hit limits regularly, when the time saved clearly exceeds the cost, or when you''re working with business data that needs proper protection. Decide based on measured time saved, not the excitement of new features.\n\nFor help choosing specific tools, see our guides to [AI tools for small businesses](/articles/ai-tools-for-small-business) and [choosing an AI writing assistant](/articles/how-to-choose-an-ai-writing-assistant).','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','Free vs Paid AI Tools: Is Upgrading Worth It?','Free vs paid AI plans compared: usage limits, model quality, data privacy, business features and hidden costs, plus a simple way to decide when to upgrade.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-22T05:59:32.319+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.411+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.411+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdp000j7ds03itk99z8','nordvpn-vs-proton-vpn-vs-purevpn','NordVPN vs Proton VPN vs PureVPN: Which VPN Should You Choose?','Three popular VPNs with very different strengths: NordVPN''s feature-packed apps, Proton VPN''s privacy-first approach and free plan, and PureVPN''s long-term value. We compare prices, renewals, privacy and features.','ai-saas',replace('NordVPN, Proton VPN and PureVPN are three of the most widely used VPN services, but they''re built for different kinds of users. This comparison uses each provider''s own published plans, policies and feature lists, so you can choose on facts rather than adverts.\n\n*Prices below are in US dollars as listed on each provider''s website in November 2026. Prices vary by country and change often, especially around sales, so always confirm the price and renewal terms at checkout.*\n\nNew to VPNs? Start with [do you really need a VPN?](/articles/do-you-need-a-vpn) and our [10-point VPN buying checklist](/articles/how-to-choose-a-vpn).\n\n## Quick comparison\n\n| | NordVPN | Proton VPN | PureVPN |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Based in | Panama | Switzerland (Geneva) | See provider''s site |\n| Free plan | No | **Yes**, no data limits | No |\n| Monthly price (1-month plan) | $14.99 (Basic) | $9.99 (VPN Plus) | See provider''s site |\n| Best-value long plan | Basic from $2.99/mo on a 2-year plan | VPN Plus from $2.99/mo on a 1-year plan | Standard from $1.49/mo on a 5-year plan |\n| Renewal price (yearly) | $139.08/yr (Basic) | $83.88/yr (VPN Plus) | $47.95/yr (Standard) |\n| Devices at once | 10 | 10 | Up to 10 |\n| Money-back guarantee | 30 days | 30 days | 31 days |\n| No-logs evidence | Independent assurance reviews by PwC and Deloitte | Says its apps are open source and independently audited | States a strict no-logs policy |\n\n## NordVPN: the feature-packed all-rounder\n\n**Who it''s for:** people who want a polished VPN with lots of extra security features and a long record of independent checks on its privacy claims.\n\n**Privacy and trust.** NordVPN operates from Panama, which it says has no data-retention laws applying to VPN providers. It states that its no-logs policy has been reviewed by PricewaterhouseCoopers AG Switzerland (2018 and 2020) and by Deloitte (2022, 2023 and 2024, with a further review in 2025), and that these assurance reports concluded it doesn''t log users'' browsing activity. That''s one of the longest track records of independent verification among consumer VPNs.\n\n**Features.** Beyond the core VPN, NordVPN lists a kill switch, split tunnelling, Double VPN (two layers of encryption), Onion Over VPN (Tor access through the VPN), Meshnet for connecting your own devices securely, and an optional Dedicated IP.\n\n**Plans.** Three tiers:\n- **Basic:** the VPN itself.\n- **Complete:** adds next-generation antivirus, scam and spam protection, ad and tracker blocking, phishing and malware defences, a password manager, 1 TB of cloud storage and identity monitoring.\n- **Prime:** adds more identity protection on top.\n\nAll plans cover **10 devices** and include a **30-day money-back guarantee**.\n\n**Price reality check.** The headline Basic price is **$2.99/month on a 2-year plan** ($80.73 for the first 27 months). It then renews at **$139.08 per year**. The 1-year Basic plan is $4.99/month ($74.85 for the first 15 months), and the month-to-month plan is **$14.99**. Set a reminder before renewal.\n\n[See NordVPN''s current plans](offer:nordvpn "button")\n\n## Proton VPN: privacy-first, with a genuinely useful free plan\n\n**Who it''s for:** privacy-focused users, people who want to start free, and anyone who likes the idea of a Swiss provider with open-source apps.\n\n**Privacy and trust.** Proton is Swiss-based and describes its VPN as having a strict no-logs policy and open-source apps that have been independently audited. Open source means security researchers can inspect the app code, which is a meaningful transparency advantage.\n\n**Free plan.** Proton offers a free plan with **no data limits** and no adverts, which is rare among reputable providers. It has fewer features and a more limited choice of server locations than the paid plan, but it''s a safe way to try a VPN without paying.\n\n**Paid plans.**\n- **VPN Plus** lists 20,000+ servers in 140+ countries, streaming and P2P support, the NetShield ad and malware blocker, and protection for **10 devices**. It costs **$9.99 month-to-month**, or **$2.99/month on a 1-year plan** (billed $35.88 for the first 12 months, then renewing at $83.88 per year).\n- **Proton Unlimited** bundles the VPN with Proton''s encrypted email, password manager and cloud storage. It costs $6.49/month on a 1-year plan ($77.88 for the first year, then $119.88 per year).\n\nBoth paid plans include a **30-day money-back guarantee**.\n\n[Try Proton VPN (free plan available)](offer:protonvpn "button")\n\n## PureVPN: long-term value with lots of add-ons\n\n**Who it''s for:** budget-conscious users happy to commit to a long plan, and people who need extras such as a dedicated IP address.\n\n**Privacy and features.** PureVPN states that it follows a strict no-logs policy. It lists an internet kill switch, DNS leak protection through its own private encrypted DNS servers, obfuscation servers, and optional Dedicated IP and dedicated server add-ons. You can protect up to **10 devices** on one account.\n\n**Plans.** PureVPN sells **Standard**, **Plus** and **Max** plans. Its plan pages list extras such as a tracker blocker, a password manager, data removal and dark web monitoring, so compare exactly what each tier includes at checkout. Its lowest advertised price is the **Standard 5-year plan at $1.49/month**, billed $93.87 for the first 63 months (5 years plus 3 bonus months). It then renews at **$47.95 per year**. The 2-year Standard plan is billed $58.20 for 27 months, with the same renewal price. Every plan has a **31-day money-back guarantee**.\n\n**Watch out for:** long commitments. A 5-year plan is excellent value if you''re sure you''ll keep using it, but test the service thoroughly within the 31-day refund window first.\n\n[Check PureVPN''s current deals](offer:purevpn "button")\n\n## Which should you choose?\n\n- **Choose NordVPN** if you want the most complete package of extra security features and the strongest track record of repeated independent no-logs reviews, and you''re happy to pay a bit more at renewal.\n- **Choose Proton VPN** if privacy and transparency matter most, if you want to start with a no-cost plan, or if you''d value bundling encrypted email and storage later.\n- **Choose PureVPN** if your priority is the lowest long-term cost and you''re comfortable committing to a multi-year plan after testing it.\n\n## Before you buy: three quick checks\n\n1. **Look at the renewal price**, not just the first-term price. All three show it at checkout.\n2. **Test during the money-back window** on every device you''ll use. Our [VPN setup guide](/articles/how-to-set-up-a-vpn) includes a two-minute leak test.\n3. **Turn on the kill switch** as soon as you install the app.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThere isn''t a single "best VPN", but there is a best fit. NordVPN leads on features and audit history, Proton VPN on transparency and its free plan, and PureVPN on long-term price. Use the money-back guarantee to test your choice on your own connection before committing to a long plan.\n\n*WiseCompare may earn a commission if you buy through links on this page, at no extra cost to you. This doesn''t affect our comparisons. See our [affiliate disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure).*','\n',char(10)),'published',1,'','NordVPN vs Proton VPN vs PureVPN: Honest Comparison','Compare NordVPN, Proton VPN and PureVPN on price, renewal cost, no-logs evidence, devices, refunds and features, and see which VPN suits you best.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-24T03:30:00.000+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.413+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.413+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdr000k7ds0thpwu2cp','incogni-review','Incogni Review: Can It Really Remove Your Personal Data from Data Brokers?','Data brokers collect and sell your name, address and phone number. Incogni promises to make them delete it. Here''s how it works, what it costs, what it can''t do, and whether it''s worth paying for.','ai-saas',replace('If you''ve ever searched your own name online and found your address, phone number or relatives listed on a "people search" website, you''ve met the data broker industry. Incogni is a subscription service that sends removal requests to these companies on your behalf. This review explains how it works, what it costs and, just as importantly, what it can''t do.\n\n*Details below come from Incogni''s official website as of November 2026. Prices are in US dollars and may vary by region.*\n\n## What are data brokers?\n\nData brokers are companies that collect personal information from public records, websites, apps and other sources. They combine it into profiles and sell it to marketers, other businesses and sometimes anyone who pays. **People search sites** are the most visible type: they let anyone look up a person''s address, phone number, relatives and more from just a name.\n\nThis data fuels spam calls and emails, targeted scams and, in the worst cases, stalking or identity theft. Many privacy laws give you the right to ask brokers to delete your data, but there are hundreds of brokers, and each has its own process.\n\n## How Incogni works\n\n1. **You sign up and authorise Incogni** to act on your behalf, providing the details brokers need to find your records (such as your name and address).\n2. **Incogni sends opt-out and removal requests** to the data brokers it covers. It says it covers **420+ data brokers**, including private brokers that don''t publish their databases.\n3. **It scans people search sites** for your information and requests takedowns where it finds matches.\n4. **It keeps repeating requests.** On annual plans, Incogni describes year-round monitoring and resending of removal requests, because brokers often re-collect data over time.\n5. **You track progress** in a dashboard showing each broker and its expected removal time.\n\nHow long removals take depends on each broker. Incogni says some respond within hours and others take weeks.\n\n## Plans and pricing\n\n| Plan | What you get | Price |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Standard** | Automatic removal requests to every broker Incogni covers | $7.99/month billed yearly ($95.88/year), or $15.98 month-to-month |\n| **Unlimited** | Standard plus **Custom Removals**: you show Incogni where your data appears and its privacy specialists work to remove it, even from sites outside the standard list | Higher; see the pricing page |\n| **Family** | Data removal for you plus **4 additional members** | Higher; see the pricing page |\n| **Family Unlimited** | Family plan with Custom Removals for every member | Highest tier |\n\nEvery plan comes with a **30-day money-back guarantee**: contact its 24/7 support team within 30 days of signing up for a full refund. Support is available by email or through the contact form in your account.\n\n[See Incogni''s current plans](offer:incogni "button")\n\n## What Incogni can''t do\n\nAn honest review has to cover the limits:\n\n- **It can''t erase you from the internet.** Social media posts, news articles, government public records and information on websites that aren''t data brokers are generally outside its scope (except, on Unlimited plans, where its specialists can act on specific sites you point to).\n- **Results take time.** Expect weeks to months for the full effect, not overnight.\n- **Data can reappear.** Brokers re-collect information, which is why the service is a subscription rather than a one-off fix.\n- **You share personal details with Incogni** so it can find your records. That''s inherent to how any data removal service works, so read its privacy policy before signing up.\n\n## Incogni vs doing it yourself\n\nYou can request removal from data brokers yourself for free: most have an opt-out form. The catch is scale. With hundreds of brokers, each with a different process, plus the need to repeat requests periodically, DIY removal can take many hours a year.\n\n| | DIY | Incogni |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Cost | Free | Subscription |\n| Time | Many hours, repeated regularly | A few minutes to set up |\n| Coverage | Whatever you have time for | 420+ brokers listed |\n| Follow-up | Manual | Automatic, recurring |\n\nA sensible middle path: if you''re mainly worried about one or two people search sites showing your address, try removing yourself from those first. If you want broad, ongoing coverage without the admin, a service like Incogni makes sense.\n\n## Who should consider Incogni?\n\n- People getting **frequent spam calls, texts or emails**.\n- Anyone whose **home address is easy to find online** and who wants that reduced.\n- People who have been through a **data breach** or identity theft scare.\n- **Families** who want everyone''s information covered in one place.\n\nIt''s less useful if your main concern is old social media content or news coverage. Those need different approaches.\n\n## How it fits with other privacy tools\n\nData removal works best alongside other basics:\n\n- A **password manager** with unique passwords (see our [1Password vs Proton Pass comparison](/articles/1password-vs-proton-pass)).\n- A **VPN** on public Wi-Fi (see [do you really need a VPN?](/articles/do-you-need-a-vpn)).\n- A **credit freeze** to block new-account fraud (see [credit monitoring vs credit freeze](/articles/credit-monitoring-vs-credit-freeze)).\n\n## Bottom line\n\nIncogni automates a tedious but worthwhile privacy task: getting data brokers to delete your personal information, and making sure they keep it deleted. It won''t erase everything about you online, and results take time, but the recurring, hands-off approach is its main value. With a 30-day money-back guarantee, it''s low-risk to try, and the annual Standard plan is the sensible starting point for most individuals.\n\n*WiseCompare may earn a commission if you sign up through links on this page, at no extra cost to you. See our [affiliate disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure).*','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','Incogni Review: How It Works, Price & Limits','An honest Incogni review: how data removal works, which plans exist, what it costs, the limits you should know about, and how it compares with doing it yourself.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-11-27T03:30:00.000+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.415+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.415+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdt000l7ds0gkf21ek8','1password-vs-proton-pass','1Password vs Proton Pass: Which Password Manager Is Right for You?','1Password is the polished, family-friendly favourite; Proton Pass is the privacy-first challenger with a generous free plan and built-in email aliases. We compare features, security, prices and who each suits.','ai-saas',replace('A password manager is one of the simplest, most effective security upgrades you can make: it creates a unique, strong password for every account and fills them in for you. **1Password** and **Proton Pass** are two of the most respected options, but they take different approaches. This comparison is based on each company''s official plans and feature lists.\n\n*Prices are in US dollars as published in November 2026. Both companies run promotions, so we quote regular and renewal prices. Check the current offer at checkout.*\n\n## Quick comparison\n\n| | 1Password | Proton Pass |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Free plan | No (14-day free trial) | **Yes**: unlimited logins on unlimited devices |\n| Individual paid plan | $3.99/month, billed yearly | Pass Plus renews at $35.88/year |\n| Family plan | $5.99/month for **up to 5 people** | Pass Family for **up to 6 users** (renews at $59.88/year) |\n| Encryption | End-to-end, AES-256 plus your personal **Secret Key** | End-to-end encryption; open source and independently audited |\n| Email aliases | No | **Yes**: hide-my-email aliases (10 on the free plan) |\n| Built-in 2FA code generator | Yes | Yes |\n| Passkeys | Yes | Yes |\n| Breach alerts | Watchtower | Dark web monitoring |\n| Money-back / trial | 14-day free trial | 30-day money-back guarantee |\n\n## 1Password: polished, powerful and great for families\n\n**Security design.** 1Password encrypts your data end to end with AES 256-bit encryption. Alongside your account password, every user also has a **Secret Key**, a long random key stored on your devices. 1Password explains that this keeps your data safe even in the unlikely event that an attacker breaches 1Password''s own systems.\n\n**Standout features:**\n- **Watchtower:** alerts you to weak, reused or compromised passwords.\n- **Travel Mode:** temporarily removes selected vaults from your devices when you cross borders.\n- **Flexible sharing:** shared vaults for family members, or expiring links to share an item with anyone, even people who don''t use 1Password.\n- **Passkey support** for passwordless sign-ins.\n- **Apps for macOS, iOS, Windows, Android and Linux**, plus browser extensions for major browsers.\n- **Developer tools** (CLI, SSH keys, Git commit signing) included in personal plans, useful if you work in tech.\n\n**Plans.** Individual costs **$3.99/month** and Families **$5.99/month** (both billed yearly). Families covers up to **five people**, each with a private vault, plus shared vaults and organiser controls to help members recover their accounts. Both plans include a **14-day free trial**.\n\n[Try 1Password free for 14 days](offer:1password "button")\n\n## Proton Pass: privacy-first, with a strong free plan\n\n**Security design.** Proton Pass uses end-to-end encryption, its code is **open source**, and it''s **independently audited**. It comes from Proton, the Swiss company behind Proton Mail and Proton VPN.\n\n**Standout features:**\n- **A free plan without the usual catches:** unlimited logins, notes and cards on **unlimited devices**, with no ads.\n- **Hide-my-email aliases:** create a unique email address for each sign-up, so your real address stays private and spam is easy to cut off. The free plan includes 10; paid plans offer more.\n- **Built-in two-factor authenticator** for storing 2FA codes.\n- **Passkey support**, secure sharing of single items or whole vaults via links, and **dark web monitoring** (check which plan includes it).\n\n**Plans.** **Pass Plus** renews at **$35.88/year**, and **Pass Family** covers **up to 6 users** (renewing at $59.88/year). Introductory prices are often lower. Paid plans have a **30-day money-back guarantee**. Proton Unlimited bundles Pass with Proton Mail, VPN and Drive.\n\n[Get Proton Pass (free plan available)](offer:protonpass "button")\n\n## Which should you choose?\n\n**Choose 1Password if:**\n- You want the most polished, proven experience across every device.\n- You''re setting up a **family** and want easy account recovery and organiser controls.\n- You value features like **Travel Mode** or developer tools.\n\n**Choose Proton Pass if:**\n- You want a **free** password manager that doesn''t cripple the basics.\n- **Email aliases** appeal to you for privacy and spam control.\n- You prefer **open-source**, Swiss-based privacy tools, or already use Proton Mail or Proton VPN.\n\n## What about the free password manager in my browser?\n\nBrowsers like Chrome and Safari can save and fill passwords, and that''s far better than reusing passwords. But a dedicated password manager works across every browser and device, shares securely with family, stores more than passwords, and adds breach monitoring. If you''re currently reusing passwords, either option here is a big step up.\n\n## Switching tips\n\n1. **Export** passwords from your browser or old manager, then **import** them into the new one (both support imports).\n2. **Delete the exported file** straight afterwards. It isn''t encrypted.\n3. Change your most important passwords first: **email, banking and phone carrier**.\n4. Turn on **two-factor authentication** for your password manager account.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nBoth are excellent choices. **1Password** is the premium, family-friendly option with thoughtful extras and a proven security model. **Proton Pass** offers the best free plan here, plus privacy features like email aliases that 1Password doesn''t have. Try 1Password''s 14-day trial or start on Proton Pass''s free plan, and you''ll know within a week which suits you.\n\nFor more ways to protect yourself online, see our [Incogni data removal review](/articles/incogni-review) and our [VPN comparison](/articles/nordvpn-vs-proton-vpn-vs-purevpn).\n\n*WiseCompare may earn a commission if you sign up through links on this page, at no extra cost to you. See our [affiliate disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure).*','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','1Password vs Proton Pass: Features, Security & Price','Compare 1Password and Proton Pass on security, free plans, family sharing, email aliases, passkeys and price, and find the right password manager for you.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-12-01T03:30:00.000+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.417+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.417+00:00');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Article VALUES('cmwuabpdv000m7ds02se8jfbh','beautiful-ai-review','Beautiful.ai Review: Is This AI Presentation Maker Worth It?','Beautiful.ai uses AI and ''Smart Slides'' that automatically keep your design tidy. Here''s what it does well, what it costs, the trial and refund terms to watch, and who should (and shouldn''t) pay for it.','ai-saas',replace('Most of the time spent on presentations isn''t spent on ideas. It goes on nudging text boxes, aligning images and fixing fonts. **Beautiful.ai** aims to remove that work with AI-generated slides and "Smart Slides" that adjust their layout automatically as you add content. Here''s an honest look at what it offers and whether it''s worth paying for.\n\n*Details are from Beautiful.ai''s official website as of November 2026. Prices are in US dollars.*\n\n## What Beautiful.ai does\n\n- **AI presentation generation:** describe what you need and it drafts a presentation for you to edit.\n- **AI writing assistant, image generation and translation** inside the editor.\n- **300+ Smart Slide layouts:** slides that automatically resize, realign and rebalance as you add or remove content, so designs stay consistent.\n- **Custom branded themes:** apply your brand fonts and colours, with brand controls for teams.\n- **PowerPoint import and export** (editable), plus **PDF export** and Google Slides options.\n- **Viewer analytics:** see how people engage with presentations you share.\n- **Team features:** shared asset libraries, collaboration and brand permissions.\n\n## Pricing\n\n| Plan | Price | Best for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Pro** | **$14.50/month, billed annually** | Individuals who present regularly |\n| **Pro, single project** | **$45, billed monthly** | One-off presentations; cancel anytime |\n| **Team** | Listed at **$40/month billed annually** ($50 billed monthly) | Small teams working on brand-consistent decks |\n| **Enterprise** | Custom | Larger organisations needing onboarding and training |\n\nThe Team pricing page describes the price **per user** in one place and as **including 3 users** in another, so confirm the exact seat terms on the pricing page before you buy. Beautiful.ai also mentions **free plans for students** and discounts for education and non-profits.\n\n[Start a Beautiful.ai free trial](offer:beautifulai "button")\n\n## The trial and refund terms: read these first\n\nThis is where buyers most often get caught out, so here are the terms exactly as stated:\n\n- There''s a **14-day free trial** of Pro or Team, and a **credit card is required** to start it.\n- **Your card is charged automatically when the trial ends** unless you cancel before the 14 days are up.\n- **Monthly subscriptions are non-refundable.**\n- **Annual subscriptions** may be eligible for a partial refund only if you request it **within 24 hours** of the initial purchase or renewal.\n\n**Our advice:** if you start the trial, set a reminder for day 12 to decide. If you only need one presentation, the single-project monthly option may be cheaper than an annual plan you won''t use.\n\n## What we like\n\n- **Smart Slides genuinely save formatting time.** The design adapts as you work, which is the core reason to choose Beautiful.ai over a blank PowerPoint file.\n- **Editable PowerPoint export** means you''re not locked in: colleagues can still edit in PowerPoint.\n- **Brand controls** keep team presentations consistent.\n- **Analytics** are useful for sales and pitch decks.\n\n## What to watch out for\n\n- **Less design freedom.** Smart Slides keep things tidy by limiting how freely you can move elements. Designers who want pixel-level control may find this restrictive.\n- **AI output needs editing.** Like any AI tool, generated content is a first draft. Check facts, figures and tone.\n- **Strict refund terms** (see above).\n- **Cost versus tools you already have.** PowerPoint and Google Slides now include AI features too, and may be enough for occasional presenters.\n\n## Who should use Beautiful.ai?\n\n**A good fit for:**\n- Consultants, founders, marketers and sales teams who make **frequent client-facing decks**.\n- Small teams that need **brand consistency** without a designer.\n- Anyone who finds slide formatting slow and frustrating.\n\n**Probably not worth it for:**\n- People who present only a few times a year (try the built-in AI in PowerPoint or Google Slides first).\n- Designers who want complete layout control.\n\n## Alternatives to consider\n\n- **PowerPoint or Google Slides** with their built-in AI assistants: cheaper if you already pay for Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, but layout work stays manual.\n- **Gamma:** another popular AI presentation tool. We''ll compare it in a separate guide.\n\nFor a framework on judging any AI subscription, see [free vs paid AI tools](/articles/free-vs-paid-ai-tools) and [AI tools for small businesses](/articles/ai-tools-for-small-business).\n\n## Bottom line\n\nBeautiful.ai is worth it if you regularly create presentations and value speed and consistency over total design freedom. Smart Slides are its real strength, and editable PowerPoint export removes the lock-in worry. Just go in with eyes open on the **card-required trial and strict refund policy**: decide before day 14, and choose the single-project option if you only need one deck.\n\n*WiseCompare may earn a commission if you subscribe through links on this page, at no extra cost to you. See our [affiliate disclosure](/affiliate-disclosure).*','\n',char(10)),'published',0,'','Beautiful.ai Review: Features, Pricing & Who It''s For','An honest Beautiful.ai review: AI presentation features, Smart Slides, PowerPoint export, pricing, free trial and refund terms, plus alternatives to consider.','WiseCompare Editorial Team','Research & Reviews','Our editorial team researches software, personal finance and home insurance products using a published methodology. We review pricing pages, terms and policy documents directly, and update guides on a regular schedule.','2026-12-04T03:30:00.000+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.419+00:00','2026-11-24T05:59:32.419+00:00');
